shared date-keyed filename; fixed by giving each mode its own output path | 2026-09-02.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[concepts/handoff-doc-uncommitted-tooling-changes]] | Handoff with `memlint.py` and four supporting files left uncommitted by choice, a temp-path placement recurrence, and a cross-session \"another session may have fixed it\" check | 2026-09-02.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[connections/always-loaded-context-is-the-expensive-place-to-be-wrong]] | Wrongness, staleness, and volume are three costs on the same session-resident surface; consolidation concentrates the first unless the pointer's target is re-verified | 2026-09-02.md, 2026-09-07.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[concepts/microsoft-forms-vs-google-forms-inventory-mismatch]] | Live inventory survey runs on Microsoft Forms while project docs point at 08-13-era Google Forms; a `gws` query there returns a truthful-but-false \"no new responses\", exposed only by the raw `.xlsx` header row | 2026-09-07.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[concepts/tier-a-memory-as-the-durable-payload-of-a-verification-session]] | A verification check's payload is a trap plus a command, so it routes to Tier A memory rather than a handoff doc; reinforced by the project not being a git repo | 2026-09-07.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[connections/a-stale-pointer-resolves-cleanly-to-the-wrong-answer]] | Stale Google Form IDs, a stale DEV memory router, and a nearly-dangling AGENTS.md pointer all succeed at resolution while returning the wrong answer; check from the target's side and stamp a verification date | 2026-09-07.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09]] | Adds the unpushed `dc54c97`/`38e462b` commits and the cross-link to `DexaProject` still living as a public GitHub repo with a patient CSV | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/backup-status-ok-versus-job-count]] | Adds the 78→86 job-count drift from 2026-09-15, restating the tell in its threshold-free form (single-digit okCount = SSD absent) | 2026-09-09.md, 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n| [[concepts/error-log-archive-collision-overwrite]] | A `cp` to a date-keyed archive path destroyed three prior `.errors.log` lines (recovered from git); archive names must not collide or must append; `cp /dev/null` is the only working truncation route | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/session-transcript-tracking-repo-growth]] | Tracking session transcripts added ~368 MB to `~/.claude` and made repo size grow daily; left as an open decision because Tier B capture is a proven reconstruction fallback | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[connections/the-preservation-step-must-itself-be-verified]] | Archive-before-clear and free-run-overwrites-paid both failed by encoding a once-daily assumption into a filename; the verified tarball discipline shows why an unchecked safety net is worse than none | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/dev-thin-repo-github-consolidation]] | `~/DEV` converted to thin repo `erash11/DEV` tracking 12 router files; `/*` block-all `.gitignore` as the load-bearing line, `CLAUDE.md` deliberately kept as a workspace map, Windows box confirmed active | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/public-repo-privacy-audit-read-inside]] | 21 public repos read inside: only `B_Ellis` and `cmj-norms-app` held real athlete data; 404-as-live bug and a Vercel-not-Pages miss in the liveness check; 18 flipped private | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]] | 36 Windows/Mac conflicts collapse to 4 (26 CRLF-only, 6 Mac-newer generated); \"Mac wins\" would have discarded 324 unique forecast-log rows | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[connections/name-and-flag-are-not-the-artifact]] | Repo names, conflict flags, and cleaned exports all over- and under-include at once; treat the cheap list as a work queue, classify by kind before resolving | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[connections/newer-is-not-more-complete]] | Revisions supersede, accumulating records diverge; the correct verb is merge or append, and a smaller newer copy is the tell | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/dev-sync-encoding-mangling-phantom-conflicts]] | ~200 `.from-*` conflicts traced to a Windows save command mangling encoding and prepending BOMs; proved on `portfolio.json` (9 updates matched, nothing lost), root cause fixed, 95 BOM files left alone | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[concepts/backup-oom-kill-during-first-time-index]] | Backup OOM-killed during a 20-min first-time index of `dev-docs` (547 files); index stale not corrupt, two dead-PID locks cleared, CPU% rejected as a health signal | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[concepts/backup-manifest-symlink-false-alarm]] | `manifest-unmanaged:_DRIVE` was a symlink, not a missing repo; backup taught to skip symlinks, two real gaps added with a 0 lost / 0 changed / 2 added proof | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[concepts/dev-sync-false-missing-repos]] | Four \"not a repo\" folders were parent-tracked, five \"missing\" repos were name mismatches, two `repo-diverged` errors had self-cleared; verify before `gh repo create` | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[concepts/git-submodule-branch-pinning-decision]] | `ZoteroInjestion` made a real submodule but pinned to `mac-snapshot-2026-09-09` rather than `main`; a snapshot pin decays silently, decision deferred | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[connections/fix-the-generator-not-the-artifacts]] | Mangled sync saves, a symlink-blind manifest check, and a backup committing its own output: recurrence is the tell that the fix belongs in the generator | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[connections/a-killed-process-leaves-debris-that-blocks-the-retry]] | Dead-PID locks and orphaned indexers are not self-healing; a loud crash becomes an indefinitely quiet no-op unless the kill's leavings are enumerated | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[concepts/harness-agnostic-aios-plan]] | Multi-task Mac+Windows skills/backup plan: 42 own vs 19 third-party skills reframing, Task 4 inversion script deleted, Task 2 prune 306→208 as a reversible move, three open round-10 findings | 2026-09-12.md | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[concepts/gitignore-trailing-slash-misses-symlinks]] | A `.gitignore` pattern ending in `/` matches directories but not symlinks, so the rule silently no-ops and an automated backup re-adds the entries; plus `.gitignore` vs `.git/info/exclude` scope | 2026-09-12.md | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[concepts/backup-verification-by-restore]] | Restore test returned 57 of 61 skills, exposing a two-hop symlink chain into an unbacked-up `~/.agents/vendor-review/`; only an actual restore can measure coverage the backup's own records cannot | 2026-09-12.md | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[concepts/cross-provider-review-gate]] | A rate-limit-forced switch from Codex to Claude found the plan's biggest error; resume cannot cross providers, and a prior approval is stale after an amendment (5 new bugs in the amendment itself) | 2026-09-12.md | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[concepts/submodule-conversion-breaks-rsync-backup]] | Submodule conversion turned `.git` from directory to file; rsync cannot overwrite a destination directory with a file, failing only while the SSD is mounted; fixed by rename, `okCount: 80` | 2026-09-12.md | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[connections/a-type-change-at-the-source-breaks-the-mirror]] | Adds the Codex-skills symlink refusal as a second rsync type-change instance, making recurrence the argument for fixing the tooling | 2026-09-12.md, 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n| [[connections/change-the-checker-down-or-sideways]] | Replacing a failed check with a primitive substrate works only where a ground truth exists; otherwise the only lever is a peer with uncorrelated blind spots. | adjudicated | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[connections/the-execution-context-silently-edits-the-work-set]] | Session lifetime and mount state both silently shrink what a run attempts without producing an error, and both are fixed by moving the verdict onto a durable artifact that publishes cardinality. | adjudicated | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[connections/test-the-layer-before-blaming-the-artifact]] | A failed upload or render usually indicts the transport layer, not the file — hold the artifact fixed and remove a stage before rebuilding anything | adjudicated | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[concepts/windows-backup-recovery-inventory]] | Windows laptop had no working backup: ~15 GB single-copy, nine Disabled tasks, two triggerless ones, and the Mac's manifest installed as Windows'; `/MIR`→`/E` fix | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n| [[concepts/windows-backup-task-trigger-gotchas]] | Four Task Scheduler gotchas: `schtasks /Change` can't add a schedule, triggerless tasks show `Ready` forever, `G:` is per-user so SYSTEM writes nothing, sleeping laptops miss fixed-time triggers | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n| [[concepts/fix-from-the-working-machine-handoff]] | Partition cross-machine repair by capability: author all file edits from the working machine, hand the broken one a paste-ready instruction page and in-file \"nothing reads this yet\" notes | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n| [[concepts/verify-the-diagnosis-in-a-received-report]] | A received recovery report's findings held but one explanation didn't: `dev-sync` skips an enumerated list, not underscore-prefixed folders, so `_INBOX` was already a clean pushed repo | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n| [[concepts/unattended-cross-machine-push-collisions]] | Three Windows pushes mid-session each needing a merge, including a deliberate-looking 913-file skill audit cutting ~160→63; accepted because git retains deletions, intent still to be confirmed | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n| [[connections/every-layer-reported-healthy-except-the-destination]] | `Ready`, `status: OK`, and a backup's own records were each locally truthful while coverage failed; narrow stage predicates don't compose into end-to-end assurance | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n| [[concepts/rsync-symlink-conversion-stale-directory]] | Codex skills converted to symlinks while the SSD held real directories; rsync cannot replace a directory with a symlink, fixed by moving four folders to `_OLD_codex-skills-dirs-2026-09-15/` | 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n| [[concepts/manifest-classification-not-inferable-from-names]] | Six Baylor-sounding repos (`VALD_Performance`, `RTP_ProgressReport`, …) were personal, not work; manifest classification must be asked, not inferred | 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n| [[concepts/clear-the-error-log-only-after-a-clean-run]] | `.errors.log` archived and cleared only after a verified 0-error/86-job re-run — fixes are not their own confirmation | 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n| [[concepts/vendored-copy-drift-within-a-repo]] | `LibraryProject`'s embedded `ZoteroInjestion` one commit behind the standalone repo; `STATUS.md` missing its Next section with an unmerged Windows copy pasted at the bottom | 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n| [[concepts/mac-owned-data-pulls-sleep-lateness]] | First Mac-owned day for GPS/Perch/VALD pulls: sleep at 3:45 PM made Perch/VALD ~1 hour late (2 Perch timeouts), all exited 0; `pmset` wake declined with a missing-not-late revisit trigger | 2026-09-22.md | 2026-09-24 |\n| [[concepts/duckdb-snapshot-merge-by-primary-key]] | `load_snapshot.py` silently deleted 12,750 Mac-only 2024–2025 rows because a 2026-only check passed; rewritten test-first to merge by primary key, restored from its own pre-load backup | 2026-09-22.md | 2026-09-24 |\n| [[concepts/claude-code-stack-config-audit-2026-09-22]] | 50-session + 303-session audit: 5 zero-use plugins proposed for disabling, `: \"` in YAML breaking a whole SKILL.md header, dcg hook dead on Windows (913 failures, Mac path), skill-router 68 s worst case | 2026-09-22.md | 2026-09-24 |\n| [[concepts/dev-sync-2026-09-22-manifest-and-union-merge]] | 146-repo sync: union merge for append-only transcript logs, backup manifest not auto-discovering `TOS_Evidence_Brief`, Tier A memory not crossing machines, `compile.py` (~$1.28) left as the user's call | 2026-09-22.md | 2026-09-24 |\n| [[connections/verify-across-every-partition-the-write-touched]] | A 2026-only row check, an exit-code-only pull check, and the three-check inventory counter-example: verification scope must span every dimension the operation could change | 2026-09-22.md | 2026-09-24 |\n| [[connections/a-guard-that-cannot-run-fails-open]] | The dead Windows dcg hook and the never-fired Perch/VALD alerts both look identical to success; protective mechanisms need a liveness signal and a deliberate periodic trip | 2026-09-22.md | 2026-09-24 |\n\n\n## Related Wiki Articles\n\n### concepts/claude-memory-compiler-setup.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Claude Memory Compiler Setup\"\naliases: [claude-memory-compiler, coleam00/claude-memory-compiler, persistent memory hooks, session-start session-end pre-compact, memory compiler version skew, claude-agent-sdk version skew, FLUSH_ERROR version skew, uv lock upgrade]\ntags: [claude-code, memory, automation, infrastructure, hooks, maintenance]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-04-14.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-05-19.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-06-13.md\"\ncreated: 2026-04-14\nupdated: 2026-06-16\n---\n\n# Claude Memory Compiler Setup\n\nOn 2026-04-14, the `coleam00/claude-memory-compiler` system — the same daily-log-to-knowledge-base architecture this very knowledge base runs on — was installed to give Claude Code persistent memory across sessions. The repo was cloned to `~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/`, Python dependencies installed via `uv sync`, and the knowledge base directory structure (`daily/`, `knowledge/concepts/`, etc.) initialized. This system is Tier C of a three-tier Claude Code memory architecture; see [[concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture]] for the full picture including Tier A (native auto-memory) and Tier B (custom Stop-hook scratchpad). On 2026-06-13, a day-long failure caused by `claude-agent-sdk` version skew revealed an important operational maintenance pattern.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Three global hooks were wired into `~/.claude/settings.json`: `session-start.py` (SessionStart), `session-end.py` (SessionEnd), and `pre-compact.py` (PreCompact).\n- Hook commands invoke `uv` via its absolute path (`~/.local/bin/uv`) rather than the bare `uv` command, because Claude Code's hook execution environment may not include `~/.local/bin` on `PATH`.\n- All script invocations follow the `uv run --directory ~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler <script>` pattern, guaranteeing the correct virtualenv context regardless of the current working directory when a hook fires.\n- Hook configuration changes are not picked up live — they require a full Claude Code restart (or a `/hooks` reload) before they take effect.\n- After 6 PM local time, the `flush.py` background process automatically triggers `compile.py` to turn that day's daily log into knowledge articles, without manual intervention. Manual queries are also available via `uv run --directory ~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler python scripts/query.py \"<question>\"`.\n- **The agent itself never receives a \"session ending\" notification** — SessionEnd and PreCompact are harness-level events; the harness runs the hook shell commands, not the agent. Teardown logic must live in the hook, not in agent-side instructions.\n- **Version skew failure mode:** the memory compiler's `uv` virtualenv pins `claude-agent-sdk` to a specific version, which bundles a specific Claude Code CLI binary. When the system Claude Code auto-updates (e.g., from 2.1.92 to 2.1.177), the bundled CLI in the venv falls behind and eventually cannot parse the current `~/.claude/settings.json`, causing every `flush.py` invocation to exit immediately with a non-zero code. Symptom: 100% FLUSH_ERROR rate after a Claude Code update, despite no changes to the memory compiler itself.\n- **Fix for version skew:** `cd ~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler && uv lock --upgrade-package claude-agent-sdk && uv sync`. This upgrades only the `claude-agent-sdk` dependency and its bundled CLI to match the current system version without touching other pinned dependencies. Run this whenever FLUSH_ERRORs appear after a Claude Code auto-update.\n- **Diagnostic caution:** do NOT invoke the bundled `claude.exe` CLI directly to diagnose version skew — it spawns a real nested agent session. Instead, inspect venv package metadata (e.g., `uv pip show claude-agent-sdk`) to check the installed version against the system CLI version.\n\n## Details\n\nThe setup session focused on making the memory pipeline reliable rather than merely functional. The PATH issue with `uv` is a generalizable lesson for any Claude Code hook that shells out to a tool installed via `uv`, `pipx`, or similar user-local installers: hook processes may run with a minimal environment, so absolute paths (or explicit `PATH` exports) are safer than relying on the interactive shell's configuration. Similarly, the `uv run --directory` pattern avoids a whole class of \"wrong virtualenv\" bugs that would otherwise occur if a hook fires while Claude Code's working directory is some unrelated project.\n\nOnce restarted, the three hooks form a closed loop: `session-start.py` injects the current knowledge index and the most recent daily log into every new session's context (capped at 20,000 characters), `session-end.py` and `pre-compact.py` capture the raw conversation transcript and spawn a detached `flush.py` process, and `flush.py` decides what's worth appending to the day's daily log and, after 6 PM, kicks off `compile.py` to synthesize that log into structured `knowledge/` articles. PreCompact serves as a safety net for long sessions: in a session with multiple auto-compactions, PreCompact fires before each one, ensuring at least one compiled log exists even if the session later crashes before SessionEnd fires.\n\nThe 2026-06-13 version skew failure is operationally important. On that day, Claude Code auto-updated from approximately version 2.1.92 to 2.1.177 — 85 releases ahead — while the memory compiler's venv still bundled the older CLI via `claude-agent-sdk==0.1.56`. The newer `~/.claude/settings.json` included hooks, settings, or schemas added after version 2.1.92 that the stale bundled CLI could not parse; every `flush.py` call exited on startup without processing the session. The failure was isolated entirely to the Tier C compile layer: Tier B (the `transcript-capture.js` Stop-hook) continued capturing sessions uninterrupted because it has no dependency on `claude-agent-sdk`. Twenty-four days of continuous Tier B logs and four sessions captured on the failure day itself confirmed the architectural independence. This means that during a version-skew failure, raw session context is preserved in Tier B transcripts and can be backfilled after the fix is applied. The `uv lock --upgrade-package claude-agent-sdk && uv sync` fix is surgical — it upgrades only the affected package without touching other pinned dependencies — and is safe to apply at any time. A post-fix test flush verifies the fix before trusting that normal operation has resumed. See [[connections/memory-compiler-tier-independence-under-failure]] for the architectural insight this failure demonstrated empirically.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture]] — The three-tier framing that situates this compiler as Tier C, alongside Tier A (native auto-memory) and Tier B (custom Stop-hook per-turn scratchpad).\n- [[concepts/claude-code-environment-maintenance]] — A later (2026-06-10) maintenance pass on the same Claude Code environment, addressing plugin/MCP configuration drift in the broader tooling this memory system runs inside.\n- [[concepts/daily-onramp-automation]] — Another automation pipeline (built 2026-06-10) sharing the same token-efficiency motivation: pre-process inputs so the LLM only has to read a compact summary.\n- [[connections/memory-compiler-tier-independence-under-failure]] — Documents the 2026-06-13 empirical demonstration that Tier B (transcript capture) remained fully operational through a day-long Tier C (compiler) failure, validating the architecture's independence claim under real failure conditions.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-04-14.md]] — Initial install, hook wiring, and PATH/uv lessons from the setup session.\n- [[daily/2026-05-19.md]] — Clarification of hook firing mechanics and the \"agent never sees session ending\" architectural detail (16:53).\n- [[daily/2026-06-13.md]] — Version skew failure and fix session (17:26 Memory Flush): root cause (claude-agent-sdk==0.1.56 bundling CLI 2.1.92 vs system 2.1.177), Tier B independence confirmed (24 days continuous logs), fix via `uv lock --upgrade-package claude-agent-sdk && uv sync`, diagnostic caution about invoking bundled claude.exe.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/outlook-inbox-triage-2026-06-04.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Outlook Inbox Triage Action List (2026-06-04 Run)\"\naliases: [2026-06-04 inbox triage, Catapult renewal overdue 6-04, Playfly timecard overdue 6-04, needs-reply follow-ups 6-04, Joe Wargo Aptive 8 days stale, Nat Shattock Jun 17 presentation]\ntags: [productivity, workflow, email, communication, triage, baylor-athletics, inbox]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-06-04.md\"\ncreated: 2026-06-04\nupdated: 2026-08-07\n---\n\n# Outlook Inbox Triage Action List (2026-06-04 Run)\n\nOn 2026-06-04, a morning Outlook inbox triage produced a prioritized summary of pending communications categorized into needs-reply, follow-ups, meetings to schedule, forms owed, still-open, and FYI buckets. The run is an instance of the recurring [[concepts/inbox-triage-methodology]] and a near-continuation of the prior two days' triages; its two highest-urgency items were again both overdue obligations carried forward — a Catapult Sports renewal (4 days past its 5/31 due date) and a Playfly LLC timecard (4+ days overdue).\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Two overdue obligations as top priority:** the Catapult Sports renewal (due 5/31, now 4 days past) and the Playfly LLC timecard verification in isolved (due 5/30, 4+ days overdue) were both flagged for immediate response — the age × importance heuristic again surfacing compliance/vendor items whose consequences escalate daily.\n- **Distribution:** 7 needs-reply, 2 stale follow-ups, 2 meetings to schedule, 3 forms owed, 2 still-open threads, and 40+ FYI/newsletter items.\n- **Needs-reply items:** Nat Shattock (Jun 17 Baylor campus presentation agenda, 1 day old), Luke Bogus (Dropback two-paths-forward direction, 3 days old), Lauren Green (Baylor Volleyball onboarding/password reset, 6 days old), and Brandon Miller (career inquiry, 3 days old).\n- **Stale follow-ups:** Austin Lewis (1080 Sprint Demo confirmation, 6 days old) and Joe Wargo (Aptive Index Review meeting, now 8 days old — carried from the 7-day flag on the 6-03 run).\n- **Meetings and forms:** Laurel Proulx + Carrie (Female Athlete Pelvic Floor survey planning call) and Baylor Athletics Performance Clinic staff attendance (Courteney Tompkins) to schedule; AMHS Education summer feedback survey and PFF translations (due 2026-07-01) among forms owed; a GitHub Claude app permissions request also flagged for review.\n\n## Details\n\nThe 2026-06-04 triage is a near-continuation of the [[concepts/outlook-inbox-triage-2026-06-03]] and [[concepts/outlook-inbox-triage-2026-06-02]] runs: the Catapult renewal, Playfly timecard, 1080 Sprint demo (Austin Lewis), Joe Wargo Aptive Index, Brandon Miller career inquiry, Lauren Green onboarding, and Luke Bogus Dropback threads all recur, with overdue counters incremented (Joe Wargo 7→8 days stale, Catapult now 4 days past). This day-over-day persistence again demonstrates the methodology's core value: a categorized snapshot makes accumulating communication debt visible, and the age-based sort within each category keeps escalating obligations from being masked by the day's 40+ FYI/newsletter volume. The Catapult renewal is another manifestation of the recurring Catapult OpenField vendor friction across Baylor projects. The following day's [[concepts/outlook-inbox-triage-2026-06-05]] run carries these same threads forward with counters again incremented.\n\nThe triage was the first of several sessions that day; later sessions pivoted to OKR staff-meeting rollout prep (see [[concepts/okr-review-facilitation-framework]] and [[concepts/applied-performance-okr-system]]) and the Fall Camp macro load model design (see [[concepts/baylor-fall-camp-macro-load-model]]). A memory flush at 14:21 recorded a FLUSH_ERROR (command exit code 1), an instance of the intermittent memory-compiler flush failures documented elsewhere.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/inbox-triage-methodology]] — The structured 6-category audit methodology this run instantiates; the needs-reply/follow-ups/meetings/forms bucketing and age × importance prioritization applied here are defined there.\n- [[concepts/outlook-inbox-triage-2026-06-03]] — The prior-day run sharing nearly all of this run's threads; comparing the two shows overdue counters incrementing day over day.\n- [[concepts/outlook-inbox-triage-2026-06-05]] — The following-day run continuing the same thread set with counters again incremented.\n- [[connections/catapult-token-friction-across-baylor-projects]] — The Catapult renewal item is another instance of recurring Catapult OpenField vendor friction across Baylor projects.\n- [[concepts/okr-review-facilitation-framework]] — The OKR staff-meeting rollout prep built later the same day.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-06-04.md]] — Inbox triage session (08:14): 7 needs-reply, 2 stale follow-ups, 2 meetings to schedule, 3 forms owed, 2 still-open, 40+ FYI; Catapult renewal (4 days overdue) and Playfly timecard (4+ days overdue) flagged OVERDUE; Joe Wargo Aptive 8 days stale, PFF translations due 7/1, GitHub Claude app permissions request flagged.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/stale-error-log-archival-discipline.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Distinguishing Stale from Live Entries Before Clearing an Error Log\"\naliases: [.errors.log archival, stale error entries, error log clearing procedure, archive then clear, escalation log hygiene, live vs stale escalation]\ntags: [automation, maintenance, memory, macos, workflow, verification]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-08-31.md\"\ncreated: 2026-08-31\nupdated: 2026-09-01\n---\n\n# Distinguishing Stale from Live Entries Before Clearing an Error Log\n\nThe `~/.claude/global-memory/transcripts/.errors.log` file is the asynchronous escalation channel through which scheduled jobs surface failures to the next interactive session. Because it is append-only and read at session start, it accumulates entries from failures that have since been fixed, and a stack-doctor pass must decide per-entry whether each one still describes a live condition. On 2026-08-31 the log's entries were confirmed stale, then archived and cleared per the skill's documented procedure.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **The error log is an escalation channel, not a state record.** Scheduled tasks write to it because no session exists at run time; entries persist until manually cleared regardless of whether the underlying failure was resolved.\n- **Clearing requires a staleness determination first.** Each entry must be checked against current ground truth — the relevant job's log, the artifact it was supposed to produce — before the log is emptied, or a live failure is silently discarded.\n- **Archive, then clear.** The procedure preserves the entries elsewhere rather than deleting them outright, keeping the failure history available while restoring the log's signal value for the next session.\n- **An uncleared log degrades into noise.** Because it is surfaced at every session start, stale entries train the reader to skim past it, which is the same alarm-fatigue dynamic that motivated raising the memsearch session-end alarm threshold from 3 to 6.\n\n## Details\n\nThe escalation log exists to solve a specific structural problem: a scheduled job that fails at 06:15 or 09:00 has no session to report to, so it writes to a durable file that the next session start reads. That design makes the log write-only from the job's perspective — nothing in the pipeline knows when a failure has been remediated, so nothing removes the entry. The log therefore represents \"failures that were reported at some point,\" not \"failures that are currently true,\" and any maintenance pass over it is fundamentally a reconciliation task rather than a cleanup task.\n\nThe reconciliation is per-entry and requires an independent source. On 2026-08-31 the determination was made by checking the backup log's timeline: the entries corresponded to the 09:00 manifest failure, and the 10:31 re-run had already completed clean, so every entry described a resolved condition. Had any entry lacked such a corroborating success, clearing the log would have erased the only surviving record of an unresolved failure. Archiving before clearing hedges against a wrong staleness call — the entries remain recoverable if a later session discovers the condition was live after all — and it also preserves the failure history for pattern analysis across maintenance passes.\n\nThe cost of not doing this is not data loss but signal loss. A log that is displayed at every session start and reliably contains only old, already-fixed entries stops being read, at which point the escalation channel it implements has failed silently while appearing to work. Keeping it empty except when something is genuinely wrong is what makes its non-emptiness informative.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/automation-stack-doctor]] — Defines the stack-doctor routine and the `.errors.log` escalation mechanism that scheduled tasks write to; this article covers the maintenance of that log.\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]] — The same 2026-08-31 session; the manifest failure was the source of the entries that were confirmed stale and cleared.\n- [[concepts/memsearch-tool-maintenance]] — The alarm-threshold raise (3→6) that addressed the same noise-versus-signal problem from the opposite direction: making an alarm fire less often so that firing means something.\n- [[concepts/in-session-background-process-limits]] — Explains why scheduled jobs need an asynchronous escalation file at all: they run without a session to report to.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-08-31.md]] — Stack-doctor session (10:34): `.errors.log` entries confirmed stale rather than live, archived and cleared per the skill's documented procedure.\n\n```\n\n### connections/keep-it-retrievable-not-resident.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Connection: Cut Resident Context, Not Retrievable Content\"\nconnects:\n  - \"concepts/daily-onramp-automation\"\n  - \"concepts/system-health-check-remediation\"\ntags: [context-management, token-efficiency, automation, memory, claude-code, architecture]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-06-10.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-06-05.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-06-25.md\"\ncreated: 2026-08-30\nupdated: 2026-08-30\n---\n\n# Connection: Cut Resident Context, Not Retrievable Content\n\n## The Connection\n\nThe daily on-ramp and the 2026-06-25 system health check attacked the same cost problem — the fixed token price every session pays before any real work happens — from opposite ends of the pipeline, and converged on the same rule without either naming it.\n\nThe on-ramp attacked the **input boundary**. Rather than having the model read raw calendar and email exports each morning, `onramp.sh` pre-aggregates them and the model only reads the compact result: 536 tokens in-context versus an estimated 114.5K of raw source, cumulating to 4.0 MB avoided across 22 days at a 214× compression ratio. The raw calendar and email data was never destroyed or made unavailable — it simply stopped being something the model had to hold.\n\nThe health check attacked the **memory-injection boundary**. The global MEMORY.md Tier A index was trimmed from 15 files to 10, cutting per-session injection from ~8,800 to ~6,700 tokens. The four removed files — Zotero CLI, AI Integration Initiative, Athlete Data Governance, Sport Science Symposium — were explicitly **kept on disk and kept memsearch-searchable**. Nothing was deleted; only the always-on footprint shrank.\n\nBoth are the same move: separate *retrievable* from *resident*, and pay standing token cost only for the latter.\n\n## Key Insight\n\nThe instinct when a system costs too many tokens is to make it do less — read fewer sources, index fewer files, drop to a cheaper model. Both of these sessions rejected that and instead moved content across the resident/retrievable line while keeping the corpus intact.\n\nThe non-obvious part is what this implies about *where* the savings should be taken. Both sessions found that the expensive thing was not the work but the **standing overhead** — data sitting in context that a given session probably will not use. The on-ramp's raw email JSON and the health check's four Tier A files share a property: they are high-volume, low-hit-rate, and cheap to fetch on demand. That combination, not raw size, is the trimming criterion. A large file consulted every session belongs resident; a small file consulted monthly does not.\n\nThis also explains why a third instance of the same discipline appears in [[concepts/claude-code-environment-maintenance]] and is easy to miss as such: disconnecting the unused Supabase, Vercel, and Google Calendar MCP connectors was framed as silencing `/doctor` re-auth warnings, but the real payoff named there is eliminating \"the tool-schema token overhead of loading unused MCP tool definitions each session.\" Unused MCP tool schemas are exactly the same shape of waste — standing per-session cost for capability that is retrievable (reconnect the connector) whenever it is actually wanted. Three separate maintenance passes, three different layers — input gathering, memory injection, tool schemas — one lever.\n\nThe tension worth flagging: this principle is *not* license to shrink the quality-determining step. [[concepts/ingest-book-skill]] draws the same distinction in the other direction, arguing that downgrading the atomization model saves tokens but produces permanently inferior entries — because atomization output is resident forever and not re-fetchable. The rule generalizes as: compress what can be re-derived on demand, never what becomes permanent.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- 2026-06-10: On-ramp lean-mode pilot measured 536 tokens kept in-context against ~114.5K if raw calendar/email had been read directly — 100% of that volume avoided, logged to `onramp-audit.md`. The underlying calendar and email sources remained fully available.\n- 2026-06-05: Cumulative on-ramp savings reached 4.0 MB across 22 days / 105 conversations, a 214× compression ratio before `/compact` — confirming the saving compounds rather than being a one-time win.\n- 2026-06-25: Tier A auto-injection index trimmed 15→10 files, ~8,800→~6,700 estimated injected tokens per session; the four removed files stayed on disk and memsearch-searchable, so retrievability was explicitly preserved.\n- 2026-06-10: MCP connectors for Supabase, Vercel, and Google Calendar recommended for disconnection at the source partly to eliminate per-session tool-schema token overhead for tools not exercised from Claude Code.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/daily-onramp-automation]] — Applies the principle at the input-gathering boundary via gather-then-distill; quantified the per-run and cumulative savings.\n- [[concepts/system-health-check-remediation]] — Applies the same principle at the memory-injection boundary via Tier A index trimming, with explicit retention of memsearch retrievability.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture]] — The Tier A / Tier B split is the structural encoding of the resident-versus-retrievable distinction this connection generalizes.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-environment-maintenance]] — A third instance at the tool-schema layer: disconnecting unused MCP connectors to drop standing per-session overhead.\n- [[concepts/ingest-book-skill]] — The counterexample that bounds the rule: never compress the step whose output is permanent and non-re-derivable.\n- [[concepts/session-context-footprint-audit]] — The auditing practice that makes standing-cost decisions measurable rather than intuitive.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/dev-sync-encoding-mangling-phantom-conflicts.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Windows /dev-sync Encoding Mangling Manufactured ~200 Phantom Conflict Files\"\naliases: [.from-mac .from-windows files, ΓÇö mojibake, BOM prepended by PowerShell save, portfolio.json 9 updates matched, phantom sync conflicts, dev-sync root cause fix, 95 BOM files 36 repos]\ntags: [git, sync, windows, macos, encoding, data-integrity, powershell, gotcha]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-11.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-11\nupdated: 2026-09-12\n---\n\n# Windows /dev-sync Encoding Mangling Manufactured ~200 Phantom Conflict Files\n\nRoughly 200 `.from-mac` / `.from-windows` conflict files had accumulated under `~/DEV` from the Windows↔Mac sync workflow. Root-cause analysis on 2026-09-11 established that none of them were real disagreements: the Windows side of `/dev-sync` was saving files with a command that mangled encoding — em-dash rendered as `ΓÇö` — and prepended a byte-order mark, so git saw every touched file as changed. The fix was applied to the Windows script rather than to the conflict files it produced.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **The \"conflicts\" were an artifact of the writer, not of two editors.** A save path that re-encodes text and prepends a BOM changes every byte-sensitive comparison git makes, so files that nobody edited on either side present as divergent.\n- **The claim was proved on a single file rather than asserted.** `portfolio.json` was un-mangled and compared: all 9 apparently-missing updates matched exactly. Nothing had been lost at any point.\n- **The fix went to the root cause.** The Windows `/dev-sync` save command was corrected, so the generator stops producing conflict files, instead of only sweeping up the ~200 already on disk.\n- **95 files across 36 repos still carry BOMs and were deliberately left alone** — some PowerShell scripts genuinely require a BOM to be interpreted correctly, making a blanket strip a separate and riskier job.\n- **17 leftover `.from-*` files from the 9/9 merge were also left in place**: already committed, carrying no risk, and the 9 inside `graphify-out/` are regenerable output that can simply be deleted.\n\n## Details\n\nThis is the second consecutive recorded instance of a cross-machine conflict list that collapses almost entirely on inspection. Two days earlier, 36 flagged files reduced to 4 once 26 were identified as CRLF-only differences and 6 as newer generated artifacts. The mechanism here is adjacent but distinct: CRLF normalization makes the working tree lie toward difference as a side effect of a checkout policy, whereas encoding mangling actively rewrites file content on save. The practical consequence is the same and worth stating as a rule — a conflict *count* emitted by any sync tool measures byte divergence, which is a much weaker property than semantic divergence, and the first move on receiving such a list is to classify by kind of difference before resolving a single entry.\n\nThe discipline that distinguishes this session is that the diagnosis was verified against ground truth before the fix was applied. The hypothesis — that the Windows save path was the transforming layer — predicts that reversing the transformation should make the two copies identical. Testing that on `portfolio.json` and finding all 9 updates matching converts a plausible story into a demonstrated one, and simultaneously answers the question that actually mattered to the user, which was not \"why are these files here\" but \"did I lose anything.\" This is the same discriminating-check-before-fixing move applied when `skill-tax` was miscounting skills: two candidate explanations pointed at opposite remedies, so the check had to precede either.\n\nThe deliberate non-actions are as informative as the fix. Stripping 95 BOMs across 36 repos would touch PowerShell scripts where the BOM is load-bearing, converting a cosmetic annoyance into a functional breakage; deferring that work and recording why is the correct call. Likewise, the 17 committed `.from-*` files pose no risk, and the nine under `graphify-out/` are generated output whose Windows-side copies have no independent value. Where this diverges sharply from the 2026-09-09 forecast-log finding is that none of these files are append-only records — there is no accumulating history that a blanket deletion would silently discard, which is precisely the check that made deletion safe here and dangerous there.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]] — The 2026-09-09 predecessor: 36 conflicts collapsing to 4, and the append-only-log exception that makes blanket resolution rules dangerous.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-worktree-gotchas]] — CRLF normalization as the sibling mechanism by which a working tree reports false differences.\n- [[connections/windows-defaults-outlive-the-windows-machine]] — Windows-side encoding behaviour continuing to shape a repository long after the work moved to a Mac.\n- [[connections/ground-truth-checks-must-match-the-lying-layer]] — Identifying the transforming layer first, then checking on the far side of it; `portfolio.json` un-mangled is exactly that check.\n- [[connections/step-down-to-ground-truth-when-the-surface-lies]] — A conflict flag is a surface signal; the byte-level comparison beneath it is the ground truth.\n- [[connections/name-and-flag-are-not-the-artifact]] — A `.from-*` filename asserts a conflict that the content does not support.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-11.md]] — Memory maintenance session (13:10): ~200 `.from-*` files under `~/DEV` traced to a Windows `/dev-sync` save command that mangled encoding (em-dash → `ΓÇö`) and prepended a BOM; proved on `portfolio.json` where all 9 \"missing\" updates matched exactly after un-mangling; Windows-side root cause fixed rather than symptoms swept; 95 BOM-carrying files across 36 repos left alone because some PowerShell scripts need them. Sync session (21:41): 17 remaining `.from-*` files from the 9/9 merge left in place as already-committed and risk-free, with the 9 in `graphify-out` identified as deletable generated output.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/clipboard-image-paste-claude-code.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Pasting Clipboard Images into Claude Code\"\naliases: [Ctrl+V image paste, Cmd+V pastes text, screenshot into Claude Code, terminal image paste, drag file from Finder fallback, Cmd+Ctrl+Shift+4]\ntags: [claude-code, terminal, macos, workflow, screenshots, input]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-08-10.md\"\ncreated: 2026-08-10\nupdated: 2026-08-11\n---\n\n# Pasting Clipboard Images into Claude Code\n\nGetting a screenshot into a Claude Code session is best done through the clipboard rather than through a saved file. On macOS, capture a region directly to the clipboard with `Cmd+Ctrl+Shift+4`, then paste into Claude Code with **`Ctrl+V`** — not `Cmd+V`. This distinction was established on 2026-08-10 alongside the mouse-button screenshot configuration.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Clipboard is the recommended route** for images into Claude Code — it avoids a round trip through the filesystem and any save-location ambiguity.\n- **Paste with `Ctrl+V`, not `Cmd+V`.** In most terminal emulators `Cmd+V` pastes text only; `Ctrl+V` is the binding that carries the clipboard image through to Claude Code.\n- **Capture straight to clipboard with `Cmd+Ctrl+Shift+4`** (region capture, no file written), the macOS counterpart of `Cmd+Shift+4`.\n- **Fallback:** if the terminal does not support image paste at all, drag the image file from Finder into the session instead — which is the case where a configured screenshot save folder becomes relevant again.\n- The clipboard path and the save-to-file path serve different purposes and are worth binding to separate buttons rather than treating as alternatives to the same need.\n\n## Details\n\nThe `Cmd+V` versus `Ctrl+V` split is a terminal-layer artifact rather than a Claude Code behavior. macOS applications conventionally use `Cmd+V` for paste, and terminal emulators honor that convention for *text*, intercepting the keystroke and injecting the clipboard's text representation into the TTY. An image on the clipboard has no meaningful text representation, so `Cmd+V` either does nothing or pastes a filename. `Ctrl+V` is not intercepted by the emulator's text-paste path in the same way, allowing Claude Code's own input handling to read the clipboard's image data. The practical implication is that a user who tries the platform-conventional shortcut first will conclude image paste is unsupported, when it works fine under the other binding.\n\nBecause the clipboard is the preferred path, the screenshot workflow configured on the same day deliberately allocated two separate mouse buttons rather than one: `Cmd+Shift+4` for captures that should persist as files in a known folder, and `Cmd+Ctrl+Shift+4` for captures destined immediately for a Claude Code prompt (see [[concepts/logitech-options-plus-mouse-screenshot-binding]]). Treating these as distinct intents avoids the otherwise-common pattern of saving a file to disk, locating it, dragging it in, and leaving an orphaned artifact behind. The saved-file path retains value as the fallback for terminals without image-paste support, and as the durable record when the screenshot matters beyond the current session — which is why the save-location configuration in [[concepts/macos-screenshot-save-location]] remains worth getting right even when the clipboard is the primary route.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/logitech-options-plus-mouse-screenshot-binding]] — The `Cmd+Ctrl+Shift+4` button binding that feeds this workflow; the two-button setup exists precisely to separate clipboard captures from saved-file captures.\n- [[concepts/macos-screenshot-save-location]] — The saved-file path used as the drag-from-Finder fallback when a terminal does not support image paste.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-environment-maintenance]] — Broader Claude Code environment configuration; input-path quirks like this belong to the same class of harness-specific behaviors worth documenting rather than rediscovering.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-08-10.md]] — Screenshot workflow session (09:00): clipboard route recommended for getting screenshots into Claude Code, `Ctrl+V` (not `Cmd+V`) as the paste binding, `Cmd+Ctrl+Shift+4` for clipboard capture, drag-file-from-Finder fallback for terminals lacking image-paste support.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/stack-doctor-sweep-2026-08-11.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Stack Doctor Sweep (2026-08-11): Self-Inflicted Repo Divergence and a Lying Dry Run\"\naliases: [claude-stack-doctor sweep, repo-diverged skills, skill-tax false positive, daily-backup.ps1 dry-run bug, MEMORY.md 100 percent, SecondBrain two-writer conflict, flush-error version skew 0.2.136]\ntags: [claude-code, automation, maintenance, git, memory, powershell, gotcha]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-08-11.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-01\nupdated: 2026-09-01\n---\n\n# Stack Doctor Sweep (2026-08-11): Self-Inflicted Repo Divergence and a Lying Dry Run\n\nA `/claude-stack-doctor` run on 2026-08-11 after a three-day gap took the automation/memory stack from 8 issues to 6 (three of the remaining six being lagging indicators that clear on the next nightly backup). The sweep's most valuable findings were not the fixes themselves but three cases where the *monitoring layer* was the defect: a nightly backup that committed its own generated files and so diverged a repo every night, a `skill-tax` check measuring the wrong quantity, and a `-DryRun` flag that reported git operations it never performed.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **`repo-diverged:skills` was self-inflicted.** Six tracked files were regenerated every run (`last-weekly-run.log` plus five `__pycache__` entries), so the nightly backup committed churn and diverged the repo nightly. Untracking and gitignoring them was the sweep's highest-leverage fix.\n- **`daily-backup.ps1 -DryRun` was lying.** The `Log` calls at lines 28/38 sat *outside* the `if (-not $DryRun)` guards at 24/34, so a dry run printed \"committed / pushed\" for git operations it never ran — briefly fooling the sweep itself. Fixed to `[dry-run] would commit`.\n- **`skill-tax` was a false positive from the monitor's own logic:** it counted all 133 on-disk `SKILL.md` files with no knowledge of `skillOverrides`. Verified empirically first (0 of 72 overridden skills leak into the listing; 61 expected, 60 seen), *then* the script was patched — the two candidate fixes were opposite, so the discriminating check had to precede either.\n- **`flush-error` recurrence across 8/08 and 8/11 indicated version skew, not contention:** `claude-agent-sdk` was 26 versions behind (0.2.110 → 0.2.136); upgrading restored flushes, and the memsearch flush patch was confirmed to survive the package operation.\n- **Both sides preserved on a two-writer conflict.** Four SecondBrain morning briefings differed from committed origin versions; origin's won by default and are live, with local copies staged at `doctor-backup-20260807-135924/secondbrain-local-briefings/*.local.md` — fully reversible rather than adjudicated on the spot.\n\n## Details\n\nThe three monitoring-layer defects share a structure worth naming: each produced a confident, well-formatted signal about a system state it was not actually measuring. The dry-run bug is the purest form — logging placed outside the guard it describes means the flag's entire purpose (previewing without acting) is silently void, and the operator's trust in the preview is inversely related to its accuracy. `skill-tax` is the same failure one level up: the check counted files on disk, a proxy that was correct before `skillOverrides` existed and became wrong afterward, so a previously-completed cleanup appeared not to have landed. The correct response in that case was explicitly *not* to fix either candidate immediately, because the two possible remedies (clean up more skills, or patch the counter) pointed in opposite directions; running the discriminating check first — do overridden skills leak into the listing? — decided which. The `repo-diverged:skills` case is the automation eating its own output: a backup job that commits generated artifacts guarantees divergence on every run, making a real alarm fire nightly for a cause the alarm cannot describe.\n\nThe sweep's restraint is as instructive as its fixes. Several issues were deliberately left alone with reasons recorded: 73 unversioned skill files (a design fork — adding `~/.agents/skills` to `backup-manifest.json` was proposed but not applied), unmanaged `DEV` directories (the user's scope call), a 4-log compiler backlog (self-healing at 2/night against ~1/day, and forcing it costs real money), and `snapshot:sessions` (an rsync race against a live Codex file, not rot). Two items were escalated as genuinely not auto-fixable and left open: Outlook auth expired, so morning briefings were shipping with 0 email threads pending an interactive `reauth.sh` run, and Google Drive 154h stale, with files reaching the local `G:` cache but GoogleDriveFS not syncing. The `MEMORY.md` file was at 100% — the next write would have failed — and was consolidated to 93% by shortening wording while keeping all 16 threads, rather than dropping any. Finally, two `flush-error` session IDs were classified benign only after evidence (the IDs exist nowhere; the DEV slug holds 28 transcripts, 3 in the last 24h), on the explicit principle of not classifying by inference; and two self-corrections were made before reporting (an \"8 → 2\" claim against a raw verdict of 6, and a claim that the /doctor changes were unversioned when the nightly backup had in fact committed them).\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/automation-stack-doctor]] — The recurring health-check routine this sweep instantiates; that article's post-restart ALL CLEAR is the same tool run under different conditions.\n- [[concepts/claude-memory-compiler-setup]] — The `claude-agent-sdk` version-skew failure mode that recurred here (0.2.110 → 0.2.136), matching the documented `uv`-upgrade remedy.\n- [[concepts/committed-symlink-repo-landmine]] — The `~/.claude/skills` `git pull` that destroyed 73 files on 8/07; this sweep verified 21/21 symlinks resolve and 154 `SKILL.md` reachable after its own pull.\n- [[concepts/skill-description-budget-ceiling]] — The skill-listing budget that `skill-tax` exists to monitor; the false positive was a measurement error against that same ceiling.\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]] — Another case where a backup subsystem's status reporting was narrower or staler than the operational question being asked.\n- [[connections/status-field-scope-narrower-than-the-question]] — The general pattern the dry-run and `skill-tax` findings both instantiate.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-08-11.md]] — Stack doctor sweep (backfilled 2026-08-29 15:02): 8 → 6 verdict, `repo-diverged:skills` traced to six self-generated tracked files, `skill-tax` false positive verified empirically before patching, `daily-backup.ps1 -DryRun` logging outside its guards, `claude-agent-sdk` 26-version skew, `MEMORY.md` 100% → 93% with all 16 threads intact, SecondBrain two-writer conflict resolved by staging both sides, deliberately-deferred items with reasons, two open user-actionable items (Outlook reauth, GoogleDriveFS), and two pre-report self-corrections. Handoff at `~/.claude/global-memory/handoffs/2026-08-11-stack-doctor-sweep.md`.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/stale-memory-router-points-at-wrong-answer.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"A Router Pointing at a Wrong Answer: Stale DEV Memory After the Mac Migration\"\naliases: [project_dev-layout-and-discovery.md, DEV lived at Desktop DEV, router pointing at wrong answer, Windows-era memory claims, eight stale DEV memory files, delegation amplifies staleness]\ntags: [memory, claude-code, migration, macos, windows, gotcha, verification]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-02.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-02\nupdated: 2026-09-07\n---\n\n# A Router Pointing at a Wrong Answer: Stale DEV Memory After the Mac Migration\n\nThe sharpest finding of the 2026-09-02 memlint run was structural rather than factual. `project_dev-layout-and-discovery.md` still claimed the `DEV` directory lived at `~/Desktop/DEV` — a Windows-era layout — while `DEV/CLAUDE.md` explicitly routed all layout questions *to that file*. The wrong fact was therefore not merely present in memory; it was the designated authority on its own topic. Roughly eight DEV memory files carried similar Windows-era claims, and all of them loaded into every session.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Delegation multiplies the cost of a stale fact.** A wrong claim sitting in an unreferenced file is one bad data point; a wrong claim in a file that another document names as the canonical source is the answer to every question on that topic.\n- **The stale claim survived the platform that produced it.** `~/Desktop/DEV` was correct on Windows and became wrong on macOS, with nothing in the migration path that would notice or flag the discrepancy.\n- **Scale matters: ~8 DEV memory files carried Windows-era claims, and they loaded every session.** Unlike a stale file on disk that is read only when opened, memory-tier staleness is paid on every conversation.\n- **Establishing a canonical pointer creates an obligation to verify the target.** `DEV/CLAUDE.md`'s routing line was itself correct and useful; the defect was that nothing re-checked the pointed-at content after the host changed.\n- **This is what made stale memories the higher-priority cleanup** over the 23 broken `[[links]]`: a broken link fails loudly and resolves to nothing, while a stale router answers confidently and wrongly.\n\n## Details\n\nThe failure has the same root as the other Windows-artifact survivals catalogued in [[connections/windows-defaults-outlive-the-windows-machine]]: a fact captured at one moment on one host, frozen into a durable artifact, and never re-verified when the host changed underneath it. What distinguishes this instance is the routing layer. Canonical-source pointers are a good pattern — they prevent the same fact from being restated in five places and drifting apart — but they concentrate risk, because the correctness of every consumer now depends on the correctness of one target. The relocation discipline in [[concepts/canonical-section-relocation-inbound-pointers]] deals with the same coupling from the other direction, where moving a canonical section leaves inbound pointers dangling; here the pointer resolves fine and delivers a wrong answer, which is strictly harder to detect.\n\nMemory-tier staleness is also more expensive than ordinary file staleness because of when it is read. A stale README costs something only when someone opens it. A stale memory file that is injected at session start is consulted implicitly in every conversation, shaping paths, assumptions, and tool invocations without ever being displayed for review. Eight such files in the DEV slug means eight Windows-era premises silently in play at the start of every DEV session. That asymmetry is the concrete argument behind the session's option-A-over-option-B choice: broken `[[links]]` degrade retrieval, whereas stale facts degrade reasoning, and the latter fails silently.\n\nThe remedy implied by the finding is not a one-time correction but a verification obligation attached to the pointer itself. Wherever one document declares another the authority on a topic, the pair should be re-checked together whenever the underlying environment changes — a migration, a directory reorganization, a tool replacement. Absent that, the routing relationship converts a single stale file into a systematically wrong answer, and the confidence with which it is delivered is proportional to how well the routing was designed.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/memlint-memory-tier-cleanup]] — The session and tooling that surfaced this finding, and the fix-in-place disposition applied to it.\n- [[concepts/canonical-section-relocation-inbound-pointers]] — The complementary hazard for canonical pointers: relocation leaves inbound references dangling rather than wrong.\n- [[connections/windows-defaults-outlive-the-windows-machine]] — The broader class of Windows-era facts and settings that survived the Mac migration.\n- [[connections/install-time-capture-goes-stale-when-host-moves]] — The same freeze-at-capture-time failure applied to interpreter paths and SDK pins.\n- [[concepts/rounded-counts-in-context-documents]] — The counter-discipline for always-loaded context: record facts at a stability level that survives change, or date-stamp them.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture]] — Why memory-tier staleness is paid per session rather than per read.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-02.md]] — Memory maintenance session (13:35): `project_dev-layout-and-discovery.md` still claiming DEV lived at `~/Desktop/DEV` while `DEV/CLAUDE.md` routes layout questions to it — \"a router pointing at a wrong answer\"; ~8 DEV memory files carrying Windows-era claims loaded every session; stale memories prioritized over the 23 broken `[[links]]`.\n\n```\n\n### connections/fix-the-generator-not-the-artifacts.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Connection: Fix the Generator, Not the Artifacts It Produced\"\nconnects:\n  - \"concepts/dev-sync-encoding-mangling-phantom-conflicts\"\n  - \"concepts/backup-manifest-symlink-false-alarm\"\n  - \"concepts/stack-doctor-sweep-2026-08-11\"\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-11.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-11\nupdated: 2026-09-12\n---\n\n# Connection: Fix the Generator, Not the Artifacts It Produced\n\n## The Connection\n\nThree findings in this stack share an identical structure: a tool emitted a stream of wrong outputs, the outputs were numerous and visible, and the correct fix was one change to the tool rather than N changes to the outputs. On 2026-09-11, the Windows `/dev-sync` save command was mangling encoding and prepending BOMs, producing roughly 200 phantom conflict files — the fix was to the save command, with most of the 200 left in place as harmless. The same day, `manifest-unmanaged:_DRIVE` was recurring because the manifest check lacked symlink awareness — the fix was to teach the check about symlinks, not to add `_DRIVE` to the manifest. On 2026-08-11, `repo-diverged:skills` fired every night because the nightly backup was committing six files it regenerated on every run — the fix was to untrack and gitignore them, not to resolve the divergence nightly.\n\n## Key Insight\n\nIn each case there is a tempting per-artifact remedy that is both more obvious and strictly worse: delete the 200 conflict files, add `_DRIVE` to the manifest, resolve the divergence each morning. Each would clear the current symptoms and guarantee their return, because none of them touches the thing producing the symptoms. The diagnostic that distinguishes the two fixes is *recurrence*: a one-off wrong output is an artifact problem, but a wrong output that regenerates on a schedule is a generator problem, and the schedule is the tell. All three cases had that tell — nightly divergence, a manifest error appearing at every session start, conflict files accumulating with every sync — and in all three the count of artifacts was large enough to make the per-artifact fix feel like the serious response when it was in fact the one that would need repeating forever.\n\nThe second-order insight is about what the artifacts cost while they persist. They are not merely untidy; they degrade the channel that reported them. A `manifest-unmanaged` list that always contains `_DRIVE` trains the operator to skim a channel that also carries real coverage gaps — and the same 2026-09-11 run surfaced two genuine omissions, `KnowledgeManagement` and `SignalPlays`, alongside the permanent noise entry. A `repo-diverged:skills` alarm that fires nightly for a self-inflicted cause makes the next genuine divergence unremarkable. Fixing the generator is therefore not only cheaper in the long run; it is what restores the meaning of the signal. The exception that proves the rule is `DexaProject`, whose manifest warning is also permanent but is kept deliberately with a recorded reason — a documented exception carries information, whereas an unfixed false positive carries only noise.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- 2026-09-11 (13:10): ~200 `.from-*` conflict files traced to the Windows `/dev-sync` save command mangling encoding (`ΓÇö`) and prepending a BOM; the Windows side was fixed rather than only cleaning up symptoms, and 17 harmless leftovers plus 95 BOM-bearing files across 36 repos were deliberately left alone.\n- 2026-09-11 (13:10): `manifest-unmanaged:_DRIVE` recurring at session start resolved by teaching the backup to skip symlinks — the check's predicate was corrected rather than an exception added — while two real gaps found by the same check were added to the manifest with a 0 lost / 0 changed / 2 added proof.\n- 2026-08-11: `repo-diverged:skills` firing nightly because `daily-backup.ps1` committed six files it regenerated each run (`last-weekly-run.log` plus five `__pycache__` entries); untracking and gitignoring them was recorded as the sweep's highest-leverage fix.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/dev-sync-encoding-mangling-phantom-conflicts]]\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-symlink-false-alarm]]\n- [[concepts/stack-doctor-sweep-2026-08-11]]\n- [[connections/the-monitor-is-the-defect]] — The adjacent case where the monitor itself is wrong; here the monitor is right about a world the generator keeps corrupting.\n- [[concepts/stale-error-log-archival-discipline]] — Why a channel carrying permanent benign entries stops functioning as an escalation path.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/claude-code-environment-maintenance.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Claude Code Environment Maintenance\"\naliases: [doctor diagnostics, plugin cache migration, mcp re-auth, effort setting, github MCP plugin, GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN, gh CLI vs MCP, fable-5 model switch, advisor incompatibility, orphan temp_github folders, github MCP 401 header, OAuth fallback disabled]\ntags: [claude-code, tooling, maintenance, infrastructure]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-06-10.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-04-14.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-06-03.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-06-09.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-07-09.md\"\ncreated: 2026-06-10\nupdated: 2026-08-01\n---\n\n# Claude Code Environment Maintenance\n\nPeriodic maintenance of the Claude Code environment surfaces configuration-drift issues that accumulate silently between active sessions. Four notable maintenance passes have been documented: a 2026-06-10 session that resolved plugin cache migration, stale MCP authentications, and temp-directory accumulation; a 2026-06-03 session that diagnosed and resolved a missing `GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` for the GitHub MCP plugin; a 2026-06-09 session that switched the default model to Fable 5 and discovered that the advisor model (opus-4-8) is incompatible with Fable 5 requests; and a 2026-07-09 observation of the GitHub MCP plugin failing to reconnect with an HTTP 401 because a configured `Authorization` header disables OAuth fallback. Most issues are caught via `/doctor` and resolved through `/mcp`, `/plugins`, or targeted configuration changes.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- `/doctor` flagged 4 issues on 2026-06-10: three claude.ai MCP servers (Supabase, Vercel, Google Calendar) needing re-authentication, and the `cli-printing-press` plugin not found at its expected versioned cache path.\n- Claude Code migrated its plugin cache from a flat layout (`cache\\<plugin>\\`) to a versioned layout (`cache\\<plugin>\\<plugin>\\<version>\\`), likely due to a Claude Code update. Plugins installed under the old layout fail version checks even though their skills may still work via a fallback path; the fix is to reinstall via `/plugins`.\n- Four stale `temp_github_*` directories were found in the plugins cache, left over from interrupted installs in late May/early June — safe to delete once confirmed unused. Interrupted plugin downloads can both lose a plugin's cache entry and leave orphan `temp_github_*` folders; `/plugin` reinstall + `/reload-plugins` restores the cache, and the orphan folders require manual deletion.\n- For MCP servers not actively used from Claude Code (Supabase, Vercel, Google Calendar), disconnecting them in claude.ai's connector settings permanently silences `/doctor` re-auth warnings and eliminates the tool-schema token overhead of loading unused MCP tool definitions each session.\n- The `compound-engineering` plugin was installed via `/plugin` and activated with `/reload-plugins`, adding `ce-*` skills/agents for planning, code review, commits, and PR workflows. `/effort` was set to \"high\" as the new session default.\n- **GitHub MCP plugin vs. `gh` CLI (2026-06-03):** the `github@claude-plugins-official` MCP plugin requires a `GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` environment variable to pass `/doctor`. If all GitHub operations use the `gh` CLI directly (authenticated via `gh auth login`; scopes: gist, read:org, repo, workflow), the MCP plugin is redundant — the cleanest fix is disabling it with `\"github@claude-plugins-official\": false` in `.claude/settings.json`. Alternatively, set `GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` from an existing PAT (e.g., reuse `CODEX_GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` if it has matching scopes).\n- **Fable 5 + medium effort + advisor incompatibility (2026-06-09):** default model switched to Fable 5, effort set to medium. The advisor model (opus-4-8) is incompatible with the Fable 5 request model — requests return 400 errors — and was disabled. When switching request models, the advisor model must also be verified for compatibility or disabled; mismatched advisor+request combinations produce API errors that are not immediately obvious as a configuration conflict.\n- **GitHub MCP 401 with Authorization header disables OAuth (2026-07-09):** the `plugin:github:github` MCP server failed to reconnect with HTTP 401 — the server rejected the configured `Authorization` header, and OAuth fallback is disabled whenever `headers.Authorization` is explicitly set. Fix: check/refresh the token, or remove the `headers.Authorization` config entirely to re-enable OAuth negotiation.\n\n## Details\n\nThe `/doctor` command acts as a quick health check across MCP connections, plugin installs, and cache state. On 2026-06-10 it caught a cache-format mismatch that was not the user's fault: a Claude Code update changed how plugins are cached on disk (from a flat directory per plugin to a versioned subdirectory), and `cli-printing-press` had been installed before that change. The plugin's files were technically present, but the version-check logic looked in the new versioned path and didn't find them — the recommended fix was simply to reinstall the plugin via `/plugins` so it lands in the new layout. The MCP re-auth findings (Supabase, Vercel, Google Calendar) were treated as low priority: rather than re-authenticating connectors that may not be exercised from Claude Code at all, the suggested path was to disconnect them at the source (claude.ai connector settings), which both stops the recurring `/doctor` warning and reduces the standing tool-schema footprint in every session.\n\nOn 2026-06-03, a separate `/doctor` run flagged the `github@claude-plugins-official` MCP server as invalid — specifically, no `GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` environment variable was set. Investigation confirmed that all GitHub interactions in this environment already flow through the `gh` CLI (authenticated separately in `~/.config/gh/`), making the GitHub MCP plugin effectively unused. The two resolution options are: (1) disable the plugin entirely in `.claude/settings.json` — the simplest fix when the plugin genuinely adds no value; or (2) set `GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` using an existing PAT with compatible scopes, avoiding a new token generation. In this case, `CODEX_GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` already existed with sufficient scopes, so `setx GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN <value>` was used. Note: `setx` writes to the Windows registry immediately but requires fully restarting VS Code for the change to propagate to the Claude Code process — see [[concepts/windows-setx-env-propagation]] for the stale-environment gotcha this can trigger.\n\nOn 2026-06-09, a maintenance session switched the default model from Sonnet-tier to Fable 5 and changed effort to medium. The advisor feature, which provides secondary-model review of responses, uses a separately-specified model (opus-4-8 in this configuration). When the request model is Fable 5, the opus-4-8 advisor returns 400 API errors because the two models are incompatible in the advisor/request pairing. The advisor was disabled as a result. The lesson: model switches require verifying the full model configuration stack — not just the primary request model but also any secondary models (advisor, sub-agent defaults, tool-use overrides) that may have incompatible pairings.\n\nOn 2026-07-09, the GitHub MCP plugin surfaced a distinct failure mode from the 2026-06-03 missing-token case: here a token/header *was* configured, but the plugin failed to reconnect with HTTP 401 because the server rejected the supplied `Authorization` header. The non-obvious mechanism is that setting `headers.Authorization` explicitly disables the plugin's OAuth fallback — the plugin assumes a manually-supplied header means the user wants header-based auth exclusively, so it does not fall back to OAuth negotiation when the header is rejected. The result is a hard 401 with no automatic recovery. The two fixes are to refresh the token behind the header, or to remove the `headers.Authorization` config so OAuth negotiation is re-enabled. This reinforces the broader 2026-06-03 finding that the GitHub MCP plugin is often redundant when `gh` CLI already handles all GitHub operations — disabling the plugin outright sidesteps both the missing-token and the header-401 failure modes.\n\nTogether these maintenance passes confirm the same operational pattern: the `/doctor` command's value is proportional to how regularly it is run and how promptly its findings are triaged. Issues like the plugin cache migration, the unused GitHub MCP plugin, the advisor incompatibility, the header-401 reconnect failure, and the orphan temp folders can accumulate silently for weeks without causing visible failures, but each unresolved warning represents either a real reliability risk or unnecessary token overhead that degrades session quality.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/custom-claude-code-skills]] — Skill creation later on the same 2026-06-10 maintenance day depended on plugins and skills registering correctly, which this maintenance pass helped ensure.\n- [[concepts/daily-onramp-automation]] — The on-ramp automation pilot run later on 2026-06-10 operates inside this same Claude Code harness.\n- [[concepts/claude-memory-compiler-setup]] — An earlier (2026-04-14) setup of the persistent-memory hook system that lives in the same `~/.claude/` configuration this maintenance pass audited.\n- [[concepts/windows-setx-env-propagation]] — The Windows registry env-var propagation gotcha encountered while applying the `GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` fix on 2026-06-03.\n- [[concepts/book-advisor-system]] — The `/advisor` invocation on 2026-07-09 that surfaced the GitHub MCP 401 also confirmed the current advisor library state (one persona).\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-06-10.md]] — `/doctor` diagnostics, plugin cache fix, MCP re-auth guidance, `compound-engineering` plugin install, `/effort` default change.\n- [[daily/2026-04-14.md]] — Source of the earlier memory-compiler hook setup referenced above.\n- [[daily/2026-06-03.md]] — GitHub MCP plugin `/doctor` warning, `gh` CLI vs. MCP investigation, `CODEX_GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` reuse via `setx`, and stale VS Code environment diagnosis (~15:00 session).\n- [[daily/2026-06-09.md]] — Fable 5 model switch (14:07): effort set to medium, opus-4-8 advisor disabled due to 400 API incompatibility with Fable 5, orphan `temp_github_*` folders confirmed and deleted.\n- [[daily/2026-07-09.md]] — GitHub MCP 401 reconnect failure (09:35): server rejects configured `Authorization` header, OAuth fallback disabled when `headers.Authorization` is set; fix is token refresh or header removal.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Claude Code Three-Tier Memory Architecture\"\naliases: [three-tier memory, Tier A memory, Tier B scratchpad, Stop hook per-turn, memory tiers, claude memory layers, hook firing mechanics, Tier B near cap, project memory files, memsearch, user_career_timeline, memory routing matrix, what not to store in memory]\ntags: [claude-code, memory, automation, hooks, infrastructure, architecture]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-05-19.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-06-05.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-06-18.md\"\ncreated: 2026-05-19\nupdated: 2026-08-07\n---\n\n# Claude Code Three-Tier Memory Architecture\n\nThe Claude Code memory system is composed of three independent layers, each operating at a different granularity and owned by a different mechanism. This architecture was articulated on 2026-05-19 during a session that traced exactly when and how each capture mechanism fires. On 2026-06-05, a practical capacity-management routing pattern emerged for when Tier B global memory approaches its character cap. On 2026-06-18, a worked example clarified how to route a specific piece of context across the tiers — and which content should not enter the memory system at all.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Tier A — Native Claude Code auto-memory:** Built into Claude Code itself; managed per-project; no user configuration required. Operates automatically within a project context; its exact retention and synthesis mechanics are not user-visible.\n- **Tier B — Custom global scratchpad via Stop hook:** A user-configured hook on the `Stop` event, which fires after every conversation turn. This provides incremental, per-turn transcript capture to a global scratchpad file (MEMORY.md) — a granularity that neither Tier A nor Tier C provides.\n- **Tier C — claude-memory-compiler:** The [[concepts/claude-memory-compiler-setup]] system: `SessionEnd` and `PreCompact` hooks fire shell commands at session teardown or auto-compaction, spawning a detached `flush.py` process that appends structured notes to the daily log; at end-of-day these are compiled into knowledge articles.\n- **The agent never sees a \"session ending\" event.** Session end and compaction are harness-level events; it is the Claude Code harness that runs the hook shell commands, not the agent itself.\n- **PreCompact as a safety net:** In a long session with multiple auto-compactions, PreCompact fires before each one, preventing total context loss if the session later crashes before `SessionEnd`.\n- **When Tier B is near cap, route to Tier A project files:** The global MEMORY.md (Tier B) has a character-cap constraint. When it approaches capacity, new durable facts should be saved to Tier A project-specific memory files (e.g., `project_<name>.md`, `user_career_timeline.md`), which are cross-project searchable via memsearch.\n- **Content-to-tier routing matrix (2026-06-18):** file locations and versioning decisions belong in Tier B as an active-thread entry (\"continue this next session\"); durable personal conventions (e.g. \"always save v1 before editing\") belong in Tier A as a feedback rule; and the actual artifact content (slides, scripts) should NOT be duplicated in memory at all because it already lives on disk. Tier C's daily-log capture provides a compressed \"what did I do that day\" summary but is not a substitute for a durable artifact reference.\n\n## Details\n\nThe three tiers are designed to capture context at different time horizons and granularities, and they operate independently rather than hierarchically. Tier A is fully opaque to the user. Tier B's Stop-hook scratchpad is the most granular: because the Stop event fires after every conversation turn, Tier B can capture mid-session context incrementally in near-real-time. Tier C produces the richest synthesized output — compiled knowledge articles from daily logs — but fires only at session boundaries.\n\nThe independence of the three tiers is their key reliability property. A failure in one does not cascade to the others: if a session crashes before `SessionEnd` fires, the Stop-hook's Tier B scratchpad may still contain the session's important turns. The \"agent never sees session ending\" detail is architecturally important: code written inside the agent context cannot detect or react to its own session ending. Teardown logic must be placed in hook shell commands that the harness executes.\n\nThe Tier B near-cap routing pattern (2026-06-05) addresses a practical capacity constraint. The correct response when Tier B is near capacity is not to compress existing entries — which risks discarding information encoded in current phrasing — but to route new durable facts into named Tier A project memory files, which are surfaced by memsearch across all project contexts.\n\nThe 2026-06-18 worked example makes the routing decision concrete and, importantly, identifies content that should stay out of memory entirely. Asked how best to preserve a working session's context (an RTP presentation's file locations, a v1/v2 versioning decision, and the slides/script themselves), the assistant produced a routing matrix rather than dumping everything into one tier: the file paths and versioning state map to Tier B because they describe an active thread to resume next session; a general preference like \"always save v1 before editing\" maps to Tier A as a durable feedback rule; and the actual slide and script content maps to neither — it already exists as files on disk, so copying it into memory would only create a stale duplicate. The distinction matters because the memory system's value is in cheap-to-retrieve pointers and rules, not in re-storing artifacts that a file path already addresses. The same example noted that Tier C's automatic daily-log capture is a lossy summary (\"good for what did I do that day, not great for pick up exactly where I left off\"), reinforcing that a deliberate Tier B pointer is the right tool when the goal is precise session continuity.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/claude-memory-compiler-setup]] — Detailed installation and configuration guide for Tier C.\n- [[concepts/daily-onramp-automation]] — A downstream consumer of Tier C's compiled daily-log output.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-environment-maintenance]] — Maintenance pass on the same Claude Code environment that houses all three memory tiers.\n- [[concepts/end-of-day-structured-interview]] — Documents the USER.md 1,375-char cap and the Tier A vs. Tier B (durable vs. active) distinction.\n- [[concepts/weekly-review-synthesis-workflow]] — The end-of-week close-out workflow where near-cap Tier B → Tier A routing was first observed in practice.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-05-19.md]] — Three-tier architecture explanation, hook firing order, and \"agent never sees session ending\" clarification (16:53).\n- [[daily/2026-06-05.md]] — Tier B near-cap → Tier A project files routing pattern observed during end-of-week memory housekeeping (18:21).\n- [[daily/2026-06-18.md]] — Content-to-tier routing worked example (01:29): file locations/versioning → Tier B active thread, \"always save v1\" → Tier A feedback rule, actual artifact content → left on disk, not duplicated in memory.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/custom-claude-code-skills.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Custom Claude Code Skills: web-scraping, ingest-resource, improve-system\"\naliases: [web-scraping skill, ingest-resource skill, improve-system skill, write-a-skill process, SecondBrain vault]\ntags: [claude-code, skills, automation, second-brain, web-scraping]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-06-10.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-04-21.md\"\ncreated: 2026-06-10\nupdated: 2026-06-15\n---\n\n# Custom Claude Code Skills: web-scraping, ingest-resource, improve-system\n\nThree custom Claude Code skills - `/web-scraping`, `/ingest-resource`, and `/improve-system` - were built in a single session on 2026-06-10 using the standard \"write-a-skill\" process, and registered under `~\\.claude\\skills\\`. The vault-facing skills were tailored to the user's actual SecondBrain vault structure rather than a generic structure inferred from prompt screenshots.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- `/web-scraping` routes by source type: free WebFetch first for static pages, Exa for semantic/discovery search, and Firecrawl v2 for JS-heavy/SPA pages; implemented as stdlib-only Python scripts (`exa_search.py`, `firecrawl_scrape.py`) that compile and fail cleanly when API keys are missing.\n- `/ingest-resource` performs tiered acquisition (WebFetch, then falling back to web-scraping's Firecrawl script) and routes content by \"shelf life\": stable reference material goes to a new `Knowledge/` folder, active-work material goes to the existing `Projects/` folder, and `Notes/` (interview-me territory) and `Inbox/` are left untouched.\n- `/improve-system` offers 5 modes - Audit, Skill Review, Experience, Historical Review, and Foundation - selected via signal-phrase routing; Audit mode archives rather than deletes and reports before touching anything, while Foundation mode hands off to `starter-voice-profile`, `starter-icp-positioning`, and `starter-design-tokens` skills.\n- Both vault-facing skills were adapted to the real SecondBrain vault at `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\SecondBrain\\`, including its existing frontmatter conventions and wikilink style.\n- Verified API contracts: Exa uses `POST /search` with an `x-api-key` header; Firecrawl v2 uses `POST /v2/scrape` with Bearer auth. Real API keys still need to be added to `~\\.claude\\skills\\web-scraping\\.env` (currently commented placeholders).\n\n## Details\n\nThe three skills were designed to compose with each other rather than operate in isolation: `/ingest-resource` calls `/web-scraping`'s Firecrawl script as a fallback when WebFetch alone can't retrieve a page, and `/improve-system` delegates to the `starter-*` skills for its Foundation mode rather than reimplementing that logic. This composition-first design means improvements to one skill (e.g., a new scraping technique added to `/web-scraping`) can propagate to the others automatically.\n\nNewly written skills register immediately in the available-skills list as long as their frontmatter is valid - the user confirmed this by running `/reload-skills` and `/reload-plugins` after writing all three. The remaining open task is operational rather than design-related: the `.env` file for `/web-scraping` still contains commented placeholder keys for Exa and Firecrawl, and a live smoke test of both APIs was suggested once real keys are added.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/claude-code-environment-maintenance]] - Skill creation followed the same day's plugin/skill registration maintenance.\n- [[concepts/catapult-connect-api-reference]] - A scraping technique discovered later that day (the ReadMe `.md`-suffix trick) is a candidate addition to `/web-scraping` and `/ingest-resource`'s routing logic.\n- [[connections/web-scraping-skill-and-readme-doc-trick]] - Explores the gap between this skill's routing tiers and the technique that actually worked for Catapult's documentation site.\n- [[connections/obsidian-vault-as-knowledge-hub]] - Connects this session's SecondBrain vault-facing skills to an earlier (2026-04-21) decision to treat an Obsidian vault as the destination for graphify's research-synthesis output from SportScienceResearchRepo.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-06-10.md]] - Skill design, API verification, and registration session.\n- [[daily/2026-04-21.md]] - Source of the earlier (2026-04-21) Obsidian-vault-as-destination decision referenced in [[connections/obsidian-vault-as-knowledge-hub]].\n\n```\n\n### concepts/dev-sync-2026-09-22-manifest-and-union-merge.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"DEV Sync 2026-09-22: Union-Merge Transcripts, Manifest Non-Discovery, and Cross-Machine Memory\"\naliases: [union merge transcripts, TOS_Evidence_Brief missing from manifest, 146 repos walked, Linda Projects space breaks sync step, Tier A does not cross machines, backup-manifest.json.bak-2026-09-22, 22 active 117 dormant]\ntags: [git, sync, backup, macos, memory, workflow, verification]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-22.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-22\nupdated: 2026-09-24\n---\n\n# DEV Sync 2026-09-22: Union-Merge Transcripts, Manifest Non-Discovery, and Cross-Machine Memory\n\nA `/dev-sync` run on 2026-09-22 walked 146 repositories to bring the Mac in line with GitHub: two pulled (`_INBOX`, `DataBase_GPS_Reporting`), one pushed (`CommandCentre`), one cloned (`TOS_Evidence_Brief`, created on Windows 9/21), with 22 active and 117 dormant-and-clean, and no conflicts. Its durable findings concern how recurring cross-machine friction is resolved: a merge strategy for append-only logs, a manifest that does not discover its own new inputs, and the fact that Tier A memory does not travel between machines.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Union merge is the right strategy for append-only transcript logs.** Mac/Windows conflicts in `global-memory/transcripts/*.md` resolve cleanly by keeping every line from both machines, producing no `.from-*` files to clean up afterward.\n- **The backup manifest does not auto-discover new repos.** `TOS_Evidence_Brief` — created on Windows the previous day — was absent from `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.json`, which made the 09:05 backup report an error. Adding it fixed the report (backup of the prior file at `backup-manifest.json.bak-2026-09-22`).\n- **Tier A memory does not cross machines.** Durable GPS decisions were therefore written into the repo's own `docs/STATUS.md` *and* `global-memory/MEMORY.md` so the Windows machine would see them.\n- **A folder name containing a space (`DEV/Linda Projects`) broke a sync step again** and had to be redone with the path quoted — a recurrence of the same hazard seen on 2026-09-11.\n- **`compile.py` was deliberately not run.** A compile costs ~$1.28, so clearing the 2-log memory-compiler backlog was left as the user's call rather than taken as an auto-action; the warning persists in `.errors.log` until then.\n\n## Details\n\nThe union-merge finding names a resolution strategy that fits the artifact's semantics rather than a generic \"take one side\" rule. A transcript log is append-only and each machine appends independently, so the two versions are not competing revisions of the same content — they are disjoint additions to a shared record. Under that structure the correct merge keeps every line, and the usual conflict-file machinery (`.from-*` copies awaiting manual adjudication) is pure overhead because there is nothing to adjudicate. This is the same classify-by-kind-before-resolving discipline applied in [[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]], where treating accumulating records as revisions would have discarded 324 unique forecast-log rows, and the same accumulate-versus-supersede distinction generalized in [[connections/newer-is-not-more-complete]]. Notably, the same day's PFF loader bug was the identical error one layer down — a replace where a merge was required.\n\nThe manifest gap is a recurrence rather than a novelty, and its recurrence is the point. A repo created on one machine does not announce itself to a backup manifest maintained on another; the manifest's coverage is declared, not discovered, so a new repo is invisible to it until a human adds a line. The 09:05 backup error was in this case an honest signal — the manifest check did fire — but the general hazard is the one catalogued in [[connections/silent-omission-defeats-per-stage-success]] and [[connections/aggregate-success-hides-a-shrunken-work-set]]: every managed item succeeds while the unmanaged item is never counted. Prior instances at [[concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09]] and [[concepts/dev-folder-manifest-default-deny]] make this at least the third occurrence, which by the reasoning in [[connections/fix-the-generator-not-the-artifacts]] is the threshold at which the fix belongs in the discovery mechanism rather than in another manual manifest edit.\n\nThe Tier A observation is a routing constraint with immediate practical force. Tier A memory is local to the machine that wrote it, so any decision that a second machine must honor — here, that the Mac owns the pulls and the Windows GPS writer is stopped — cannot live there alone. Writing it into the repository's `docs/STATUS.md` puts it in a location git already replicates, and mirroring it into `global-memory/MEMORY.md` reaches the synced memory surface; between them the decision arrives on the other machine by two independent paths. This extends the content-to-tier routing matrix in [[concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture]] with a cross-machine axis, and matches the working-machine-authors-the-fix pattern in [[concepts/fix-from-the-working-machine-handoff]]. Finally, the deliberate refusal to run `compile.py` is a recorded non-action rather than an oversight: a ~$1.28 spend is a user decision, so the backlog warning was left standing in `.errors.log` with the command documented in the compiler's README — consistent with treating `.errors.log` as an escalation channel that clears only on a verified clean run.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]] — The prior conflict triage that established classifying by kind before resolving; union merge is the resolution for the accumulating-record kind.\n- [[connections/newer-is-not-more-complete]] — Why an append-only log must be merged rather than overwritten.\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09]] — An earlier instance of the same declared-not-discovered manifest gap.\n- [[concepts/dev-folder-manifest-default-deny]] — The fail-closed manifest guard designed against exactly this omission.\n- [[connections/aggregate-success-hides-a-shrunken-work-set]] — The general shape: an unlisted item cannot fail a check it was never part of.\n- [[concepts/dev-sync-false-missing-repos]] — The 2026-09-11 sync where `Linda Projects`' embedded space first broke the parallel fetch loop.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture]] — The tier-routing matrix this session extends with a cross-machine constraint.\n- [[concepts/compiler-backlog-hook-notice]] — The backlog warning deliberately left in `.errors.log`.\n- [[concepts/clear-the-error-log-only-after-a-clean-run]] — Why the warning stays until a verified clean compile, not until the fix is merely intended.\n- [[concepts/mac-owned-data-pulls-sleep-lateness]] — The GPS decisions whose cross-machine visibility motivated the dual write.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-22.md]] — Sync and closeout session (17:25): `/dev-sync` walking 146 repos with `_INBOX` and `DataBase_GPS_Reporting` pulled, `CommandCentre` pushed, `TOS_Evidence_Brief` cloned, 22 active / 117 dormant / no conflicts; union merge resolving Mac/Windows transcript-log conflicts without `.from-*` files; `TOS_Evidence_Brief` missing from `backup-manifest.json` causing the 09:05 backup error, fixed with a `.bak-2026-09-22` backup; `DEV/Linda Projects` space breaking a sync step; Tier A memory not crossing machines, resolved by writing to `docs/STATUS.md` plus `global-memory/MEMORY.md`; `compile.py` (~$1.28) deliberately left as the user's call.\n\n```\n\nThese are the 13 articles (of 291) most related to this log, plus graph neighbours and least-linked articles. This is the relevant context, not a partial view to be cautious about.\n- The Current Wiki Index above lists EVERY article. Consult it before creating a new one, and you may [[wikilink]] any article listed there even if its text is not shown.\n- If this log adds detail, correction, or a new example to an article shown above, UPDATE it: re-emit it at its existing path with the full revised content. Updating existing articles is a primary expected outcome, not a fallback.\n\n## Daily Log to Compile\n\n**File:** 2026-09-24.md\n\n# Daily Log: 2026-09-24\n\n## Sessions\n\n## Memory Maintenance\n\n### Memory Flush (19:47)\n\nFLUSH_OK - Nothing worth saving from this session\n\n\n\n## Your Task\n\nDo NOT use any tools and do NOT attempt to write files — your sandbox cannot\nwrite under this directory. Instead, emit the articles as plain text using the\nexact delimiter format below. The orchestrator parses your output and writes the\nfiles itself.\n\n### Output format\n\nFor each new or updated article, emit a block:\n\n===ARTICLE concepts/<slug>.md===\n<full markdown: complete YAML frontmatter + body, per the AGENTS.md schema>\n===END ARTICLE===\n\nUse `connections/<slug>.md` for connection articles and `qa/<slug>.md` for Q&A.\nSlugs are lowercase and hyphenated. To UPDATE an existing article, emit it with\nits existing path and the full revised content (it overwrites).\n\nAfter the article blocks, emit exactly one index block and one log block:\n\n===INDEX===\n| [[concepts/<slug>]] | one-line summary | 2026-09-24.md | 2026-09-24 |\n===END INDEX===\n\n===LOG===\n## [2026-09-24T19:50:13-05:00] compile | 2026-09-24.md\n- Source: daily/2026-09-24.md\n- Articles created: [[concepts/<slug>]]\n- Articles updated: (none)\n===END LOG===\n\n### Rules\n1. Identify 3-7 distinct concepts worth their own article.\n2. Emit ===NOTHING=== ONLY when the log itself contains no durable knowledge\n   (e.g. \"FLUSH_OK\", or purely transient chatter). \"An article already covers\n   this topic\" is NOT a reason to emit ===NOTHING=== - it is a reason to UPDATE\n   that article with whatever this log adds.\n3. Every article: complete YAML frontmatter (title, sources, created, updated)\n   with `sources` citing 2026-09-24.md; at least 2 [[wikilinks]]; Key Points\n   3-5 bullets; Details 2+ paragraphs; Related Concepts 2+ entries; encyclopedia\n   style — neutral and comprehensive. Follow the AGENTS.md article format exactly.\n4. Create a connection article when the log reveals a non-obvious relationship\n   between 2+ concepts.\n4b. Do NOT re-emit an article whose only change would be the `updated:` date or\n   a restatement of what it already says. Measured on a real run, 4 of 5\n   \"updates\" changed nothing but the timestamp - that is churn: it rewrites\n   files, dirties backups, and invalidates caches while adding no knowledge.\n   Re-emit an article only when you are adding substantive content.\n5. Add one ===INDEX=== table row per article (new or updated).\n\nEmit ONLY the delimiter blocks — no preamble, no commentary.\n"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"dequeue","timestamp":"2026-09-25T00:50:14.421Z","sessionId":"ef352ba8-b7e9-4a99-84cf-f07c9b50ff46"}
{"parentUuid":null,"isSidechain":false,"promptId":"0b72fc11-ea8a-4d3f-ba09-1dc7ce63b8fa","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"You are a knowledge compiler. Your job is to read a daily conversation log\nand extract knowledge into structured wiki articles.\n\n## Schema (AGENTS.md)\n\n# AGENTS.md - Personal Knowledge Base Schema\n\n> Adapted from [Andrej Karpathy's LLM Knowledge Base](https://gist.github.com/karpathy/442a6bf555914893e9891c11519de94f) architecture.\n> Instead of ingesting external articles, this system compiles knowledge from your own AI conversations.\n\n## The Compiler Analogy\n\n```\ndaily/          = source code    (your conversations - the raw material)\nLLM             = compiler       (extracts and organizes knowledge)\nknowledge/      = executable     (structured, queryable knowledge base)\nlint            = test suite     (health checks for consistency)\nmemlint         = test suite     (same, for the memory files loaded each session)\nqueries         = runtime        (using the knowledge)\n```\n\nYou don't manually organize your knowledge. You have conversations, and the LLM handles the synthesis, cross-referencing, and maintenance.\n\n---\n\n## Architecture\n\n### Layer 1: `daily/` - Conversation Logs (Immutable Source)\n\nDaily logs capture what happened in your AI coding sessions. These are the \"raw sources\" - append-only, never edited after the fact.\n\n```\ndaily/\n├── 2026-04-01.md\n├── 2026-04-02.md\n├── ...\n```\n\nEach file follows this format:\n\n```markdown\n# Daily Log: YYYY-MM-DD\n\n## Sessions\n\n### Session (HH:MM) - Brief Title\n\n**Context:** What the user was working on.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- User asked about X, assistant explained Y\n- Decided to use Z approach because...\n- Discovered that W doesn't work when...\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- Chose library X over Y because...\n- Architecture: went with pattern Z\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- Always do X before Y to avoid...\n- The gotcha with Z is that...\n\n**Action Items:**\n- [ ] Follow up on X\n- [ ] Refactor Y when time permits\n```\n\n### Layer 2: `knowledge/` - Compiled Knowledge (LLM-Owned)\n\nThe LLM owns this directory entirely. Humans read it but rarely edit it directly.\n\n```\nknowledge/\n├── index.md              # Master catalog - every article with one-line summary\n├── log.md                # Append-only chronological build log\n├── concepts/             # Atomic knowledge articles\n├── connections/          # Cross-cutting insights linking 2+ concepts\n└── qa/                   # Filed query answers (compounding knowledge)\n```\n\n### Layer 3: This File (AGENTS.md)\n\nThe schema that tells the LLM how to compile and maintain the knowledge base. This is the \"compiler specification.\"\n\n---\n\n## Structural Files\n\n### `knowledge/index.md` - Master Catalog\n\nA table listing every knowledge article. This is the primary retrieval mechanism - the LLM reads this FIRST when answering any query, then selects relevant articles to read in full.\n\nFormat:\n\n```markdown\n# Knowledge Base Index\n\n| Article | Summary | Compiled From | Updated |\n|---------|---------|---------------|---------|\n| [[concepts/supabase-auth]] | Row-level security patterns and JWT gotchas | daily/2026-04-02.md | 2026-04-02 |\n| [[connections/auth-and-webhooks]] | Token verification patterns shared across Supabase auth and Stripe webhooks | daily/2026-04-02.md, daily/2026-04-04.md | 2026-04-04 |\n```\n\n### `knowledge/log.md` - Build Log\n\nAppend-only chronological record of every compile, query, and lint operation.\n\nFormat:\n\n```markdown\n# Build Log\n\n## [2026-04-01T14:30:00] compile | Daily Log 2026-04-01\n- Source: daily/2026-04-01.md\n- Articles created: [[concepts/nextjs-project-structure]], [[concepts/tailwind-setup]]\n- Articles updated: (none)\n\n## [2026-04-02T09:00:00] query | \"How do I handle auth redirects?\"\n- Consulted: [[concepts/supabase-auth]], [[concepts/nextjs-middleware]]\n- Filed to: [[qa/auth-redirect-handling]]\n```\n\n---\n\n## Article Formats\n\n### Concept Articles (`knowledge/concepts/`)\n\nOne article per atomic piece of knowledge. These are facts, patterns, decisions, preferences, and lessons extracted from your conversations.\n\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Concept Name\"\naliases: [alternate-name, abbreviation]\ntags: [domain, topic]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-04-01.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-04-03.md\"\ncreated: 2026-04-01\nupdated: 2026-04-03\n---\n\n# Concept Name\n\n[2-4 sentence core explanation]\n\n## Key Points\n\n- [Bullet points, each self-contained]\n\n## Details\n\n[Deeper explanation, encyclopedia-style paragraphs]\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/related-concept]] - How it connects\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-04-01.md]] - Initial discovery during project setup\n- [[daily/2026-04-03.md]] - Updated after debugging session\n```\n\n### Connection Articles (`knowledge/connections/`)\n\nCross-cutting synthesis linking 2+ concepts. Created when a conversation reveals a non-obvious relationship.\n\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Connection: X and Y\"\nconnects:\n  - \"concepts/concept-x\"\n  - \"concepts/concept-y\"\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-04-04.md\"\ncreated: 2026-04-04\nupdated: 2026-04-04\n---\n\n# Connection: X and Y\n\n## The Connection\n\n[What links these concepts]\n\n## Key Insight\n\n[The non-obvious relationship discovered]\n\n## Evidence\n\n[Specific examples from conversations]\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/concept-x]]\n- [[concepts/concept-y]]\n```\n\n### Q&A Articles (`knowledge/qa/`)\n\nFiled answers from queries. Every complex question answered by the system can be permanently stored, making future queries smarter.\n\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Q: Original Question\"\nquestion: \"The exact question asked\"\nconsulted:\n  - \"concepts/article-1\"\n  - \"concepts/article-2\"\nfiled: 2026-04-05\n---\n\n# Q: Original Question\n\n## Answer\n\n[The synthesized answer with [[wikilinks]] to sources]\n\n## Sources Consulted\n\n- [[concepts/article-1]] - Relevant because...\n- [[concepts/article-2]] - Provided context on...\n\n## Follow-Up Questions\n\n- What about edge case X?\n- How does this change if Y?\n```\n\n---\n\n## Core Operations\n\n### 1. Compile (daily/ -> knowledge/)\n\nWhen processing a daily log:\n\n1. Read the daily log file\n2. Read `knowledge/index.md` to understand current knowledge state\n3. Read existing articles that may need updating\n4. For each piece of knowledge found in the log:\n   - If an existing concept article covers this topic: UPDATE it with new information, add the daily log as a source\n   - If it's a new topic: CREATE a new `concepts/` article\n5. If the log reveals a non-obvious connection between 2+ existing concepts: CREATE a `connections/` article\n6. UPDATE `knowledge/index.md` with new/modified entries\n7. APPEND to `knowledge/log.md`\n\n**Important guidelines:**\n- A single daily log may touch 3-10 knowledge articles\n- Prefer updating existing articles over creating near-duplicates\n- Use Obsidian-style `[[wikilinks]]` with full relative paths from knowledge/\n- Write in encyclopedia style - factual, concise, self-contained\n- Every article must have YAML frontmatter\n- Every article must link back to its source daily logs\n\n### 2. Query (Ask the Knowledge Base)\n\n1. Read `knowledge/index.md` (the master catalog)\n2. Based on the question, identify 3-10 relevant articles from the index\n3. Read those articles in full\n4. Synthesize an answer with `[[wikilink]]` citations\n5. If `--file-back` is specified: create a `knowledge/qa/` article and update index.md and log.md\n\n**Why this works without RAG:** At personal knowledge base scale (50-500 articles), the LLM reading a structured index outperforms cosine similarity. The LLM understands what the question is really asking and selects pages accordingly. Embeddings find similar words; the LLM finds relevant concepts.\n\n### 3. Lint (Health Checks)\n\nSeven checks, run periodically:\n\n1. **Broken links** - `[[wikilinks]]` pointing to non-existent articles\n2. **Orphan pages** - Articles with zero inbound links from other articles\n3. **Orphan sources** - Daily logs that haven't been compiled yet\n4. **Stale articles** - Source daily log changed since article was last compiled\n5. **Contradictions** - Conflicting claims across articles (requires LLM judgment)\n6. **Missing backlinks** - A links to B but B doesn't link back to A\n7. **Sparse articles** - Below 200 words, likely incomplete\n\nOutput: a markdown report with severity levels (error, warning, suggestion).\n\n---\n\n## Conventions\n\n- **Wikilinks:** Use Obsidian-style `[[path/to/article]]` without `.md` extension\n- **Writing style:** Encyclopedia-style, factual, third-person where appropriate\n- **Dates:** ISO 8601 (YYYY-MM-DD for dates, full ISO for timestamps in log.md)\n- **File naming:** lowercase, hyphens for spaces (e.g., `supabase-row-level-security.md`)\n- **Frontmatter:** Every article must have YAML frontmatter with at minimum: title, sources, created, updated\n- **Sources:** Always link back to the daily log(s) that contributed to an article\n\n---\n\n## Full Project Structure\n\n```\nllm-personal-kb/\n|-- .claude/\n|   |-- settings.json                # Hook configuration (auto-activates in Claude Code)\n|-- .gitignore                       # Excludes runtime state, temp files, caches\n|-- AGENTS.md                        # This file - schema + full technical reference\n|-- README.md                        # Concise overview + quick start\n|-- pyproject.toml                   # Dependencies (at root so hooks can find it)\n|-- daily/                           # \"Source code\" - conversation logs (immutable)\n|-- knowledge/                       # \"Executable\" - compiled knowledge (LLM-owned)\n|   |-- index.md                     #   Master catalog - THE retrieval mechanism\n|   |-- log.md                       #   Append-only build log\n|   |-- concepts/                    #   Atomic knowledge articles\n|   |-- connections/                 #   Cross-cutting insights linking 2+ concepts\n|   |-- qa/                          #   Filed query answers (compounding knowledge)\n|-- scripts/                         # CLI tools\n|   |-- compile.py                   #   Compile daily logs -> knowledge articles\n|   |-- query.py                     #   Ask questions (index-guided, no RAG)\n|   |-- lint.py                      #   7 health checks (knowledge base)\n|   |-- memlint.py                   #   6 health checks (memory tier)\n|   |-- flush.py                     #   Extract memories from conversations (background)\n|   |-- config.py                    #   Path constants\n|   |-- utils.py                     #   Shared helpers\n|-- hooks/                           # Claude Code hooks\n|   |-- session-start.py             #   Injects knowledge into every session\n|   |-- session-end.py               #   Extracts conversation -> daily log\n|   |-- pre-compact.py               #   Safety net: captures context before compaction\n|-- reports/                         # Lint reports (gitignored)\n```\n\n---\n\n## Hook System (Automatic Capture)\n\nHooks are configured in `.claude/settings.json` and fire automatically when you use Claude Code in this project.\n\n### `.claude/settings.json` Format\n\n```json\n{\n  \"hooks\": {\n    \"SessionStart\": [{ \"matcher\": \"\", \"hooks\": [{ \"type\": \"command\", \"command\": \"uv run python hooks/session-start.py\", \"timeout\": 15 }] }],\n    \"PreCompact\": [{ \"matcher\": \"\", \"hooks\": [{ \"type\": \"command\", \"command\": \"uv run python hooks/pre-compact.py\", \"timeout\": 10 }] }],\n    \"SessionEnd\": [{ \"matcher\": \"\", \"hooks\": [{ \"type\": \"command\", \"command\": \"uv run python hooks/session-end.py\", \"timeout\": 10 }] }]\n  }\n}\n```\n\nCommands use simple relative paths from the project root. Empty `matcher` catches all events.\n\n### Hook Details\n\n**`session-start.py`** (SessionStart)\n- Pure local I/O, no API calls, runs in under 1 second\n- Reads `knowledge/index.md` and the most recent daily log\n- Outputs JSON to stdout: `{\"hookSpecificOutput\": {\"hookEventName\": \"SessionStart\", \"additionalContext\": \"...\"}}`\n- Claude sees the knowledge base index at the start of every session\n- Max context: 20,000 characters\n\n**`session-end.py`** (SessionEnd)\n- Reads hook input from stdin (JSON with `session_id`, `transcript_path`, `cwd`)\n- Copies the raw JSONL transcript to a temp file (no parsing in the hook - keeps it fast)\n- Spawns `flush.py` as a fully detached background process\n- Recursion guard: exits immediately if `CLAUDE_INVOKED_BY` env var is set\n\n**`pre-compact.py`** (PreCompact)\n- Same architecture as session-end.py\n- Fires before Claude Code auto-compacts the context window\n- Guards against empty `transcript_path` (known Claude Code bug #13668)\n- Critical for long sessions: captures context before summarization discards it\n\n**Why both PreCompact and SessionEnd?** Long-running sessions may trigger multiple auto-compactions before you close the session. Without PreCompact, intermediate context is lost to summarization before SessionEnd ever fires.\n\n### Background Flush Process (`flush.py`)\n\nSpawned by both hooks as a fully detached background process:\n- **Windows:** `CREATE_NEW_PROCESS_GROUP | DETACHED_PROCESS` flags\n- **Mac/Linux:** `start_new_session=True`\n\nThis ensures flush.py survives after Claude Code's hook process exits.\n\n**What flush.py does:**\n1. Sets `CLAUDE_INVOKED_BY=memory_flush` env var (prevents recursive hook firing)\n2. Reads the pre-extracted conversation context from the temp `.md` file\n3. Skips if context is empty or if same session was flushed within 60 seconds (deduplication)\n4. Calls Claude Agent SDK (`query()` with `allowed_tools=[]`, `max_turns=2`)\n5. Claude decides what's worth saving - returns structured bullet points or `FLUSH_OK`\n6. Appends result to `daily/YYYY-MM-DD.md`\n7. Cleans up temp context file\n8. **End-of-day auto-compilation:** If it's past 6 PM local time (`COMPILE_AFTER_HOUR = 18`) and today's daily log has changed since its last compilation (hash comparison against `state.json`), spawns `compile.py` as another detached background process. This means compilation happens automatically once a day without needing a cron job or manual trigger.\n\n### JSONL Transcript Format\n\nClaude Code stores conversations as `.jsonl` files. Messages are nested under a `message` key:\n\n```python\nentry = json.loads(line)\nmsg = entry.get(\"message\", {})\nrole = msg.get(\"role\", \"\")     # \"user\" or \"assistant\"\ncontent = msg.get(\"content\", \"\")  # string or list of content blocks\n```\n\nContent can be a string or a list of blocks (`{\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"...\"}` dicts).\n\n---\n\n## Script Details\n\n### compile.py - The Compiler\n\nUses the Claude Agent SDK's async streaming `query()`:\n\n```python\nasync for message in query(\n    prompt=compile_prompt,\n    options=ClaudeAgentOptions(\n        cwd=str(ROOT_DIR),\n        system_prompt={\"type\": \"preset\", \"preset\": \"claude_code\"},\n        allowed_tools=[\"Read\", \"Write\", \"Edit\", \"Glob\", \"Grep\"],\n        permission_mode=\"acceptEdits\",\n        max_turns=30,\n    ),\n):\n```\n\n- Builds a prompt with: AGENTS.md schema, current index, all existing articles, and the daily log\n- Claude reads the daily log, decides what concepts to extract, and writes files directly\n- `permission_mode=\"acceptEdits\"` auto-approves all file operations\n- Incremental: tracks SHA-256 hashes of daily logs in `state.json`, skips unchanged files\n- Cost: ~$0.45-0.65 per daily log (increases as KB grows)\n\n**CLI:**\n```bash\nuv run python scripts/compile.py              # compile new/changed only\nuv run python scripts/compile.py --all        # force recompile everything\nuv run python scripts/compile.py --file daily/2026-04-01.md\nuv run python scripts/compile.py --dry-run\n```\n\n### query.py - Index-Guided Retrieval\n\nLoads the entire knowledge base into context (index + all articles). No RAG.\n\nAt personal KB scale (50-500 articles), the LLM reading a structured index outperforms vector similarity. The LLM understands what you're really asking; cosine similarity just finds similar words.\n\n**CLI:**\n```bash\nuv run python scripts/query.py \"What auth patterns do I use?\"\nuv run python scripts/query.py \"What's my error handling strategy?\" --file-back\n```\n\nWith `--file-back`, creates a Q&A article in `knowledge/qa/` and updates the index and log. This is the compounding loop - every question makes the KB smarter.\n\n### lint.py - Health Checks\n\nSeven checks:\n\n| Check | Type | Catches |\n|-------|------|---------|\n| Broken links | Structural | `[[wikilinks]]` to non-existent articles |\n| Orphan pages | Structural | Articles with zero inbound links |\n| Orphan sources | Structural | Daily logs not yet compiled |\n| Stale articles | Structural | Source logs changed since compilation |\n| Missing backlinks | Structural | A links to B but B doesn't link back |\n| Sparse articles | Structural | Under 200 words |\n| Contradictions | LLM | Conflicting claims across articles |\n\n**CLI:**\n```bash\nuv run python scripts/lint.py                    # all checks\nuv run python scripts/lint.py --structural-only  # skip LLM check (free)\n```\n\nReports saved to `reports/lint-YYYY-MM-DD.md`.\n\n### memlint.py - Memory Tier Health Checks\n\n`lint.py` stops at `knowledge/`. The files the harness actually injects into every\nsession live elsewhere and were never audited: `~/.claude/global-memory/` and the\nactive `~/.claude/projects/*/memory/` slugs. Drift there is the most expensive\ndrift in the system, because it is read at the start of every conversation.\n\nSeven checks:\n\n| Check | Type | Catches |\n|-------|------|---------|\n| Index drift | Structural | Memory file missing from MEMORY.md, or an index pointer with no file |\n| Broken links | Structural | `[[links]]` that do not match any frontmatter `name:` in the slug |\n| Name form | Structural | `name:` that is not the bare kebab stem (type belongs in `metadata.type`) |\n| Near-duplicates | Structural | Within-slug TF-IDF similarity above the threshold |\n| Cap overruns | Structural | A file past the character cap it declares in its own header |\n| Content in index | Structural | Facts written into MEMORY.md, which is meant to hold pointers |\n| Contradictions | LLM | Conflicting, redundant, or superseded claims across memory files |\n\n**Scope.** Retired `C--Users-eric-rash*` slugs are excluded everywhere - the macOS\nslugs were re-keyed from them, so including them reports history as duplication.\n`handoffs/` and `transcripts/` are session records, not memory, and are never linted.\nThe LLM check defaults to `global-memory` + the `DEV` and `~` slugs, the ones that\nload together in a working session; `--all-slugs` widens it.\n\n**Similarity threshold.** Default 0.45. Measured 2026-09-01: the highest genuine\nwithin-slug pair scored 0.33. The report always prints the top pairs per slug so\nthe number can be retuned against evidence.\n\n**Report only.** memlint never edits a memory file. Merging and deleting memories\nstays a human decision.\n\n**CLI:**\n```bash\nuv run python scripts/memlint.py                    # all checks\nuv run python scripts/memlint.py --structural-only  # skip LLM check (free)\nuv run python scripts/memlint.py --all-slugs        # widen the LLM check\nuv run python scripts/memlint.py --threshold 0.6    # retune near-duplicates\n```\n\nReports saved to `reports/memlint-YYYY-MM-DD.md`; `--structural-only` writes\n`memlint-YYYY-MM-DD-structural.md` instead, so a free run never clobbers the\nday's paid-for LLM findings. Runs automatically after the Sunday 02:30\nadjudication job.\n\n---\n\n## State Tracking\n\n`scripts/state.json` tracks:\n- `ingested` - map of daily log filenames to SHA-256 hashes, compilation timestamps, and costs\n- `query_count` - total queries run\n- `last_lint` - timestamp of most recent lint\n- `last_memlint` - timestamp of most recent memory-tier lint\n- `total_cost` - cumulative API cost\n\n`scripts/last-flush.json` tracks flush deduplication (session_id + timestamp).\n\nBoth are gitignored and regenerated automatically.\n\n---\n\n## Dependencies\n\n`pyproject.toml` (at project root):\n- `claude-agent-sdk>=0.1.29` - Claude Agent SDK for LLM calls with tool use\n- `python-dotenv>=1.0.0` - Environment variable management\n- `tzdata>=2024.1` - Timezone data\n- Python 3.12+, managed by [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/)\n\nNo API key needed - uses Claude Code's built-in credentials at `~/.claude/.credentials.json`.\n\n---\n\n## Costs\n\n| Operation | Cost |\n|-----------|------|\n| Compile one daily log | $0.45-0.65 |\n| Query (no file-back) | ~$0.15-0.25 |\n| Query (with file-back) | ~$0.25-0.40 |\n| Full lint (with contradictions) | ~$0.15-0.25 |\n| Structural lint only | $0.00 |\n| Full memlint (with contradictions) | ~$0.15-0.25 |\n| Structural memlint only | $0.00 |\n| Memory flush (per session) | ~$0.02-0.05 |\n\n---\n\n## Customization\n\n### Additional Article Types\n\nAdd directories like `people/`, `projects/`, `tools/` to `knowledge/`. Define the article format in this file (AGENTS.md) and update `utils.py`'s `list_wiki_articles()` to include them.\n\n### Obsidian Integration\n\nThe knowledge base is pure markdown with `[[wikilinks]]` - works natively in Obsidian. Point a vault at `knowledge/` for graph view, backlinks, and search.\n\n### Scaling Beyond Index-Guided Retrieval\n\nAt ~2,000+ articles / ~2M+ tokens, the index becomes too large for the context window. At that point, add hybrid RAG (keyword + semantic search) as a retrieval layer before the LLM. See Karpathy's recommendation of `qmd` by Tobi Lutke for search at scale.\n\n\n## Current Wiki Index\n\n# Knowledge Base Index\n\n| Article | Summary | Compiled From | Updated |\n|---------|---------|---------------|---------|\n| [[concepts/claude-code-environment-maintenance]] | Adds 2026-07-09 GitHub MCP 401-on-reconnect: configured Authorization header disables OAuth fallback; fix is token refresh or header removal | 2026-06-10.md, 2026-04-14.md, 2026-06-03.md, 2026-06-09.md, 2026-07-09.md | 2026-08-01 |\n| [[concepts/custom-claude-code-skills]] | Three new skills (web-scraping, ingest-resource, improve-system) built for the SecondBrain vault; now cross-linked to an earlier Obsidian-vault-as-destination decision | 2026-06-10.md, 2026-04-21.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/daily-onramp-automation]] | Lean-mode on-ramp pipeline; now includes 2026-06-05 sparse-input run and cumulative ctx savings (4.0 MB / 22 days / 105 conversations, 214× compression before /compact) | 2026-06-10.md, 2026-04-14.md, 2026-06-05.md | 2026-06-16 |\n| [[concepts/catapult-connect-api-reference]] | Local Catapult Connect v6 API reference; now cross-linked to a mirror-image Perch API auth-scheme lesson from 2026-04-25 | 2026-06-10.md, 2026-04-14.md, 2026-04-25.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/keystone-os-tezino-project]] | KeystoneOS Tezino Phase 1 data-gap closure (CMJ/IMTP backfill, mRSI decline, Catapult token failure); now cross-linked to downstream Tezino_CampPrep SPC monitoring project initiated 2026-06-09 | 2026-06-10.md, 2026-04-14.md, 2026-04-25.md, 2026-06-09.md | 2026-06-16 |\n| [[connections/catapult-api-docs-and-tezino-token-issue]] | Same-day link between the Catapult API reference's token docs and the Tezino sync's stale-token failure | 2026-06-10.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[connections/web-scraping-skill-and-readme-doc-trick]] | Gap between the new web-scraping skill's routing tiers and the ReadMe .md/llms.txt technique that actually worked | 2026-06-10.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/claude-memory-compiler-setup]] | Memory compiler setup; now includes version-skew failure mode (claude-agent-sdk bundled CLI diverges from system), fix via uv lock --upgrade-package, and Tier B independence confirmed | 2026-06-13.md | 2026-06-16 |\n| [[concepts/baylor-load-simulator-deployment]] | Baylor Load Simulator Vercel deployment; now includes the 2026-05-12 Comparison View feature and Catapult period-aggregation sync findings | 2026-04-14.md, 2026-04-24.md, 2026-05-12.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/skylar-zinnecker-cmj-rtc-reports]] | CMJ return-to-competition HTML reports for athlete Skylar Zinnecker, built from a VALD ForceDecks export, with redefined baseline and \"Post-Op Low\" terminology | 2026-04-14.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[connections/catapult-token-friction-across-baylor-projects]] | Recurring Catapult OpenField token friction linking the Baylor Load Simulator deploy, the Tezino sync failure, and the Catapult API reference | 2026-04-14.md, 2026-06-10.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[connections/vald-forcedecks-cmj-data-across-projects]] | Shared VALD ForceDecks CMJ data and mRSI metric linking Skylar Zinnecker's RTC reports to the Tezino narrative backfill | 2026-04-14.md, 2026-06-10.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/sportscience-research-repo-batch-import]] | Round-7 batch import + data-quality repair; now records the \"verify prior background agents actually finished\" procedural lesson from 2026-04-22 | 2026-04-21.md, 2026-04-22.md, 2026-04-24.md | 2026-08-06 |\n| [[concepts/graphify-vault-separation]] | Decision to move graphify's 300+ generated files out of SportScienceResearchRepo and into the user's Obsidian vault | 2026-04-21.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[connections/obsidian-vault-as-knowledge-hub]] | Obsidian SecondBrain as recurring knowledge destination: graphify cleanup, /ingest-resource skill, morning briefing durable copy, and Applied Performance OKR hub as canonical project home | 2026-04-21.md, 2026-06-10.md, 2026-05-21.md, 2026-06-01.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/the-monitor-newsletter]] | \"The Monitor\" newsletter Issue 01 production: content verification, staff photo integration, preview-server rendering gotcha, and template-directory documentation | 2026-04-22.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[connections/verify-before-trusting-tool-output]] | Links a 2026-04-22 agent-citation-hallucination fix and a newsletter photo-rendering gotcha as instances of \"verify directly, don't trust the intermediate layer\" | 2026-04-22.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/perch-weight-room-domain-feature]] | Perch/Weight Room domain feature, now including its 2026-04-25 data-activation completion (436 athletes, 12,300 set records ingested via corrected Perch API integration) | 2026-04-24.md, 2026-04-25.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/subagent-driven-development]] | Two-stage review methodology (spec-compliance then code-quality) used to implement and verify Perch/Weight Room Task 9 | 2026-04-24.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/jinja2-none-safety-pattern]] | Jinja2's `default()` filter unreliably handles `None`; use explicit `is not none` guards instead | 2026-04-24.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[connections/multi-stage-review-catches-agent-blind-spots]] | Multi-stage review principle extended to planning context: spec self-review catching carryover resolution behavioral gap | 2026-04-22.md, 2026-04-24.md, 2026-05-21.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/perch-api-integration-reference]] | Verified Perch API integration details (Bearer auth, /v3/sets pagination, 1RM formula, exercise/org IDs) that completed the Weight Room domain's data-activation step | 2026-04-25.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[connections/api-credential-assumptions-can-be-backwards]] | Links a 2026-04-25 Perch auth-scheme surprise (Bearer worked, JWT didn't) to the 2026-06-10 Catapult token failure as mirror-image \"credential appearance is unreliable\" lessons | 2026-04-25.md, 2026-06-10.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/baylor-load-simulator-comparison-view]] | Projected-vs-Actual ComparisonChart on /practices/[id]/compare, planned /comparison nav tab, single-metric scope; now cross-linked to the Color Triad Intended↔Realized leg | 2026-05-12.md | 2026-08-06 |\n| [[concepts/catapu